== Grace Abounding to the Chief of Sinners

1\. In this my relation of the merciful working of God upon my soul, it will not be amiss if, in the first place, I give you, in a few words, a hint of my pedigree and manner of bringing up; so that by this the goodness and bounty of God toward me may be the more advanced and magnified before the sons of men.

2\. For my descent, then, it was, as is well known by many, of a low and inconsiderable generation, my father’s house being of that rank that is lowliest and most despised of all the families in the land. So I have nothing here, as others have, to boast of in noble blood or a high-born state according to the flesh; though, all things considered, I magnify the heavenly Majesty for this, that by this door He brought me into this world, to partake of the grace and life that is in Christ by the gospel.

3\. But yet, notwithstanding the lowliness and inconsiderableness of my parents, it pleased God to put it into their hearts to put me to school, to learn both to read and to write; which I also attained, much as other poor men’s children did; though, to my shame I confess, I soon lost that little I learned, even almost utterly, and that long before the Lord worked His gracious work of conversion upon my soul.

4\. As for my own natural life, for the time that I was without God in the world, it was indeed in keeping with “the ways of this world” and with “the spirit who is now at work in the sons of disobedience” (Eph 2:2, 3). It was my delight to be one whom the devil “has taken captive to his will” (2 Tim 2:26). I was filled with all unrighteousness, which also worked and put itself forth so strongly, both in my heart and life, and that from a child, that I had but few equals, especially considering my years, which were tender, being few, both for cursing, swearing, lying, and blaspheming the holy name of God.

5\. Indeed, I was so settled and rooted in these things that they became like a second nature to me; and this, as I have also soberly considered since, so offended the Lord that even in my childhood He scared and frightened me with fearful dreams, and terrified me with dreadful visions. For often, after I had spent this and that day in sin, I have been greatly afflicted in my bed, while asleep, with the apprehensions of devils and wicked spirits, who continually, as I then thought, labored to draw me away with them, of which I could never be rid.

6\. Also at these years I would be greatly afflicted and troubled with the thoughts of the day of judgment, and that both night and day, and would tremble at the thoughts of the fearful torments of hellfire; always fearing that it would be my lot to be found at last among those devils and hellish fiends who are bound down there with the chains and bonds of darkness, “for judgment on that great day.”

7\. These things, I say, when I was but a child about nine or ten years old, so distressed my soul that then, in the midst of my many sports and childish vanities, amid my vain companions, I was often much cast down and afflicted in my mind by them; yet I could not let go of my sins. Indeed, I was also then so overcome with despair of life and heaven that I would often wish either that there had been no hell, or #emph[that I had been a devil], supposing they were only tormentors; so that if I indeed had to go there, I might be a tormentor rather than be tormented myself.

8\. A while after, these terrible dreams left me, and I also soon forgot them; for my pleasures quickly cut off the remembrance of them, as if they had never been. So, with more greediness, according to the strength of nature, I continued to let loose the reins to my lust, and delighted in all transgression against the law of God; so that, until I came to the state of marriage, I was the very ringleader of all the youth that kept me company, in all manner of vice and ungodliness.

9\. Indeed, the lusts and fruits of the flesh had such power in this poor soul of mine that, had not a miracle of precious grace prevented it, I would not only have perished by the stroke of eternal justice, but would also have laid myself open even to the stroke of those laws which bring some to disgrace and open shame before the face of the world.

10\. In these days the thoughts of religion were very grievous to me; I could neither endure it myself, nor endure that any other should; so that, when I saw some reading in those books that concerned Christian piety, it would be as it were a prison to me. Then I said to God, “Depart from me! For I have no desire to know Your ways” (Job 21:14). I was now void of all good consideration; heaven and hell were both out of sight and mind; and as for saving and damning, they were least in my thoughts. O Lord, You know my life, and my ways were not hidden from You.

11\. But this I well remember: though I could myself sin with the greatest delight and ease, and also take pleasure in the vileness of my companions, yet even then, if I at any time saw wicked things done by those who professed goodness, it would make my spirit tremble. As once, above all the rest, when I was at the height of my vanity, I heard one swear who was reckoned a religious man, and it struck my spirit so greatly that it made my heart ache.

12\. But God did not utterly leave me, but followed me still, not now with convictions, but with judgments; yet such as were mixed with mercy. For once I fell into a creek of the sea, and hardly escaped drowning. Another time I fell out of a boat into Bedford river, but mercy still preserved me alive. Besides, another time, being in the field with one of my companions, it happened that an adder crossed the highway; so I, having a stick in my hand, struck her over the back; and having stunned her, I forced open her mouth with my stick and plucked her sting out with my fingers; by which act, had not God been merciful to me, I might, by my desperateness, have brought myself to my end.

13\. This also I have taken notice of with thanksgiving: when I was a soldier, I, with others, was drawn out to go to a certain place to besiege it; but when I was just ready to go, one of the company asked to go in my place; and when I had consented, he took my place, and coming to the siege, as he stood sentinel, he was shot in the head with a musket bullet, and died.

14\. Here, as I said, were judgments and mercy, but neither of them awakened my soul to righteousness; so I sinned still, and grew more and more rebellious against God, and careless of my own salvation.

15\. Soon after this I changed my condition into a married state, and my mercy was to light upon a wife whose father was counted godly. This woman and I, though we came together as poor as poor could be, not having so much household stuff as a dish or spoon between us both, yet she had this for her part, #emph[The Plain Man’s Pathway to Heaven] and #emph[The Practice of Piety], which her father had left her when he died. In these two books I would sometimes read with her, and in them I also found some things that were somewhat pleasing to me; but all this while I met with no conviction. She also would often be telling me what a godly man her father was, and how he would reprove and correct vice, both in his house and among his neighbors; what a strict and holy life he lived in his day, both in word and deed.

16\. So these books, with this account, though they did not reach my heart to awaken it about my sad and sinful state, yet they did beget within me some desires for religion; so that, because I knew no better, I fell in very eagerly with the religion of the times: that is, to go to church twice a day, and that too with the foremost, and there very devoutly both say and sing as others did, yet keeping my wicked life. But with that, I was so overrun with the spirit of superstition that I adored, and that with great devotion, even all things, both the high place, priest, clerk, vestments, service, and whatever else belonged to the church; counting all things holy that were contained in it, and especially the priest and clerk most happy and, without doubt, greatly blessed, because they were the servants, as I then thought, of God, and were principal in the holy temple, to do His work in it.

17\. This notion grew so strong upon my spirit in a little time that, had I but seen a priest, however sordid and debauched in his life, I would find my spirit fall under him, reverence him, and be knit to him. Indeed, I thought that, for the love I bore them, supposing they were the ministers of God, I could have lain down at their feet and been trampled on by them; their name, their garb, and their work so intoxicated and bewitched me.

18\. After I had been like this for some considerable time, another thought came into my mind; and that was, whether we were of the Israelites or not. For, finding in the Scriptures that they were once the special people of God, I thought, if I were of this race, my soul must surely be happy. Now again I found within me a great longing to be resolved about this question, but could not tell how I should be. At last I asked my father about it, who told me, No, we were not. So then my spirit fell as to the hopes of that, and so remained.

19\. But all this while I was not aware of the danger and evil of sin; I was kept from considering that sin would damn me, whatever religion I followed, unless I was found in Christ. No, I never thought of Him, nor whether there was such a one or not. Thus man, while blind, wanders, but wearies himself with vanity, for he does not know the way to the city of God (Eccl 10:15).

20\. But one day, among all the sermons our parson preached, his subject was the Sabbath day, and the evil of breaking it, whether with labor, sports, or otherwise. Now I was, notwithstanding my religion, one who took much delight in all manner of vice, and #emph[that] was especially the day on which I indulged myself in it. So I fell in my conscience under his sermon, thinking and believing that he made that sermon on purpose to show me my evil doing; and at that time I felt what guilt was, though never before, that I can remember; but then I was, for the present, greatly loaded with it, and so went home when the sermon was ended with a great burden on my spirit.

21\. This, for that instant, numbed the sinews of my best delights, and embittered my former pleasures to me; but behold, it did not last, for before I had well dined, the trouble began to go off my mind, and my heart returned to its old course. But oh, how glad I was that this trouble was gone from me, and that the fire was put out, so that I might sin again without control! So, when I had satisfied nature with my food, I shook the sermon out of my mind, and returned with great delight to my old custom of sports and gaming.

22\. But the same day, as I was in the midst of a game of cat, and having struck it one blow from the hole, just as I was about to strike it the second time, a voice suddenly darted from heaven into my soul, which said, “Will you leave your sins and go to heaven, or have your sins and go to hell?” At this I was put into an exceeding amazement; so, leaving my cat on the ground, I looked up to heaven, and it was as if I had, with the eyes of my understanding, seen the Lord Jesus looking down upon me, as being very hotly displeased with me, and as if He severely threatened me with some grievous punishment for these and my other ungodly practices.

23\. I had no sooner conceived this in my mind than suddenly this conclusion was fastened on my spirit (for the former hint set my sins again before my face), that I had been a great and grievous sinner, and that it was #emph[now too late] for me to look after heaven; for Christ would not forgive me, nor pardon my transgressions. Then I fell to musing upon this also; and while I was thinking of it, and fearing lest it should be so, I felt my heart sink in despair, concluding it was too late; and therefore I resolved in my mind that I would go on in sin. For, I thought, if the case is so, my state is surely miserable: miserable if I leave my sins, and only miserable if I follow them; I can only be damned, and if it must be so, I might as well be damned for many sins as be damned for few.

24\. Thus I stood in the midst of my play, before all who were then present; yet I told them nothing. But, I say, having made this conclusion, I returned desperately to my sport again; and I well remember that at once this kind of despair so possessed my soul that I was persuaded I could never attain any other comfort than what I should get in sin; for heaven was gone already, so that I must not think of that. So I found within me a great desire to take my fill of sin, continually studying what sin was yet to be committed, that I might taste the sweetness of it; and I made as much haste as I could to fill my belly with its delicacies, lest I should die before I had my desire; for that I feared greatly. In these things, I protest before God, I do not lie, nor do I feign this form of speech; these were really, strongly, and with all my heart my desires. The good Lord, whose mercy is unsearchable, forgive me my transgressions.

25\. And I am very confident that this temptation of the devil is more usual among poor creatures than many are aware of: to overrun their spirits with a scurvy and seared frame of heart, and a benumbing of conscience; which frame he quietly and slyly supplies with such despair that, though not much guilt attends their souls, yet they continually have a secret conclusion within them that there is no hope for them; for they have loved sins, “therefore after them they will go” (Jer 2:25; 18:12).

26\. Now therefore I went on in sin with great greediness of mind, continually grudging that I could not be as satisfied with it as I would have liked. This continued with me about a month or more. But one day, as I was standing at a neighbor’s shop window, and there cursing and swearing and playing the madman after my usual manner, the woman of the house sat within and heard me. Though she also was a very loose and ungodly wretch, yet she protested that I swore and cursed at such a most fearful rate that she was made to tremble to hear me; and she told me further that I was the ungodliest fellow for swearing that she had ever heard in all her life, and that I, by doing this, was able to spoil all the youth in the whole town, if they but came into my company.

27\. At this reproof I was silenced, and put to secret shame, and that too, as I thought, before the God of heaven. So, while I stood there hanging down my head, I wished with all my heart that I might be a little child again, that my father might teach me to speak without this wicked way of swearing; for, I thought, I am so accustomed to it that it is but in vain for me to think of a reformation, for I thought it could never be.

28\. But how it came to pass I do not know: from this time forward I so left my swearing that it was a great wonder to myself to observe it; and whereas before I did not know how to speak unless I put an oath before and another behind, to make my words have authority, now I could speak better without it, and with more pleasantness, than ever I could before. All this while I did not know Jesus Christ, nor did I leave my sports and play.

29\. But quickly after this I fell into company with a poor man who made profession of religion, who, as I then thought, talked pleasantly of the Scriptures and of the matters of religion. So, falling into some love and liking for what he said, I took myself to my Bible, and began to take great pleasure in reading, but especially in the historical part of it; for as for Paul’s epistles and such Scriptures, I could not bear them, being as yet ignorant either of the corruptions of my nature, or of my need of Jesus Christ and His worth to save me.

30\. So I fell to some outward reformation, both in my words and life, #emph[and set the commandments before me as my way to heaven]\; and I also strove to keep these commandments, and, as I thought, kept them pretty well at times, and then I would have comfort; yet now and then I would break one, and so afflict my conscience; but then I would repent, and say I was sorry for it, and promise God to do better next time, and there get help again; for then I thought I pleased God as well as any man in England.

31\. Thus I continued about a year, all which time our neighbors took me to be a very godly man, a new and religious man, and marveled much to see such a great and famous alteration in my life and manners; and indeed so it was, though I did not yet know Christ, nor grace, nor faith, nor hope; for, as I have clearly seen since, had I then died, my state would have been most fearful. Well, this, I say, continued about a twelvemonth or more.

32\. But, I say, my neighbors were amazed at this great conversion of mine, from prodigious profaneness to something like a moral life; and truly, so they well might be; for this conversion of mine was as great as for Tom of Bedlam to become a sober man. Now, therefore, they began to praise, to commend, and to speak well of me, both to my face and behind my back. Now, as they said, I had become godly; now I had become a right honest man. But oh! when I understood that these were their words and opinions of me, it pleased me mighty well. For though as yet I was nothing but a poor painted hypocrite, yet I loved to be talked of as one who was truly godly. I was proud of my godliness, and indeed I did all I did either to be seen by men or to be well spoken of by them. And thus I continued for about a twelvemonth or more.

33\. Now #emph[you] must know that before this I had taken much delight in ringing the bells; but my conscience beginning to be tender, I thought such practice was but vain, and therefore forced myself to leave it; yet my mind hankered, so I would go to the steeple-house and look on, though I dared not ring. But I thought this did not become religion either; yet I forced myself, and would still look on. But quickly after, I began to think, “What if one of the bells should fall?” Then I chose to stand under a main beam that lay across the steeple from side to side, thinking I might stand safe there; but then I would think again: should the bell fall with a swing, it might first hit the wall, and then, rebounding upon me, might kill me in spite of this beam. This made me stand in the steeple door; and now, I thought, I am safe enough; for if a bell should then fall, I can slip out behind these thick walls, and so be preserved notwithstanding.

34\. So after this I would still go to see them ring, but would not go further than the steeple door; but then it came into my head, “What if the steeple itself should fall?” And this thought, “It may fall, for all I know,” when I stood and looked on, so continually shook my mind that I dared not stand at the steeple door any longer, but was forced to flee, for fear the steeple should fall upon my head.

35\. Another thing was my dancing: I was a full year before I could quite leave that. But all this while, when I thought I kept this or that commandment, or did by word or deed anything that I thought was good, I had great peace in my conscience, and would think to myself, “God cannot help but be pleased with me now”; indeed, to tell it in my own way, I thought no man in England could please God better than I.

36\. But, poor wretch as I was, I was all this while ignorant of Jesus Christ, and going about to establish my own righteousness; and I would have perished in it, had not God in mercy shown me more of my state by nature.

37\. But one day the good providence of God cast me to Bedford, to work at my calling; and in one of the streets of that town I came where there were three or four poor women sitting at a door in the sun, talking about the things of God. And being now willing to hear them talk, I drew near to hear what they said, for I was now a lively talker myself in the matters of religion; but I may say, I heard, but I did not understand; for they were far above, out of my reach. Their talk was about a new birth, the work of God on their hearts, also how they were convinced of their miserable state by nature; they talked of how God had visited their souls with His love in the Lord Jesus, and with what words and promises they had been refreshed, comforted, and supported against the temptations of the devil. Moreover, they reasoned of the suggestions and temptations of Satan in particular, and told one another by which they had been afflicted, and how they were borne up under his assaults. They also discussed their own wretchedness of heart and their unbelief, and did despise, slight, and abhor their own righteousness, as filthy and insufficient to do them any good.

38\. And it seemed to me they spoke as if joy made them speak; they spoke with such pleasantness of Scripture language, and with such an appearance of grace in all they said, that they were to me as if they had found a new world, as if they were people who dwelt alone, and were not to be reckoned among their neighbors (Num 23:9).

39\. At this I felt my own heart begin to shake, and to suspect that my condition was bad; for I saw that in all my thoughts about religion and salvation, the new birth never entered my mind; neither did I know the comfort of the Word and promise, nor the deceitfulness and treachery of my own wicked heart. As for secret thoughts, I took no notice of them; neither did I understand what Satan’s temptations were, nor how they were to be withstood and resisted, etc.

40\. Thus, therefore, when I had heard and considered what they said, I left them and went about my work again; but their talk and discourse went with me; also my heart would stay with them, for I was greatly affected by their words, both because by them I was convinced that I lacked the true marks of a truly godly man, and also because by them I was convinced of the happy and blessed condition of him who was such a one.

41\. Therefore I would often make it my business to go again and again into the company of these poor people, for I could not stay away; and the more I went among them, the more I questioned my condition. And as I still remember, I at once found two things within me, at which I at times marveled, especially considering what a blind, ignorant, sordid, and ungodly wretch I had been just before: the one was a very great softness and tenderness of heart, which caused me to fall under the conviction of what they asserted from Scripture; and the other was a great bending in my mind to a continual meditating on them, and on all other good things which at any time I heard or read of.

42\. By these things my mind was now so turned that it lay like a horseleech at the vein, continually crying out, “Give, give” (Prov 30:15). Indeed, it was so fixed on eternity, and on the things about the kingdom of heaven (that is, so far as I knew, though as yet, God knows, I knew but little), that neither pleasures nor profits, nor persuasions nor threats, could loosen it or make it let go its hold. And though I may say it with shame, yet it is in very deed a certain truth: it would then have been as difficult for me to take my mind from heaven to earth as I have often found it since to get it again from earth to heaven.

43\. One thing I may not omit. There was a young man in our town to whom my heart had before been knit more than to any other; but he being a most wicked creature for cursing, and swearing, and whoring, I now shook him off and forsook his company. But about a quarter of a year after I had left him, I met him in a certain lane and asked him how he was; he, after his old swearing and mad way, answered that he was well. “But, Harry,” said I, “why do you swear and curse like this? What will become of you if you die in this condition?” He answered me in a great rage, “What would the devil do for company, if it were not for such as I am?”

44\. About this time I met with some Ranters’ books, put out by some of our countrymen, which books were also highly esteemed by several old professing Christians. Some of these I read, but was not able to make a judgment about them; so, as I read in them and thought upon them, feeling myself unable to judge, I would betake myself to hearty prayer in this manner: “O Lord, I am a fool, and not able to know the truth from error. Lord, do not leave me to my own blindness, either to approve of or condemn this doctrine; if it is of God, let me not despise it; if it is of the devil, let me not embrace it. Lord, I lay my soul in this matter only at Your foot; let me not be deceived, I humbly beseech You.” I had one religious intimate companion all this while, and that was the poor man I spoke of before; but about this time he also turned a most devilish Ranter, and gave himself up to all manner of filthiness, especially uncleanness. He would also deny that there was a God, angel, or spirit, and would laugh at all exhortations to sobriety. When I labored to rebuke his wickedness, he would laugh the more, and claim that he had gone through all religions and could never light on the right one till now. He told me also that in a little time I should see all professing Christians turn to the ways of the Ranters. So, abhorring those cursed principles, I left his company immediately, and became as great a stranger to him as I had been a familiar friend before.

45\. Nor was this man alone a temptation to me; but, my calling lying in the country, I happened to fall into the company of several people who, though strict in religion formerly, were also swept away by these Ranters. These would also talk with me of their ways, and condemn me as legal and dark, claiming that only those had attained to perfection who could do what they would and not sin. Oh! these temptations were suited to my flesh, I being but a young man, and my nature in its prime; but God, who had, as I hope, designed me for better things, kept me in the fear of His name, and did not allow me to accept such cursed principles. And blessed be God, who put it into my heart to cry to Him to be kept and directed, always distrusting my own wisdom; for I have since seen the very effect of that prayer, in His preserving me not only from the Ranters’ errors, but also from those that have sprung up since. The Bible was precious to me in those days.

46\. And now, it seemed to me, I began to look into the Bible with new eyes, and read as I never did before; and especially the epistles of the apostle St. Paul were sweet and pleasant to me. And indeed I was then never out of the Bible, either by reading or meditation, continually crying out to God that I might know the truth and the way to heaven and glory.

47\. And as I went on and read, I lighted on that passage, “To one there is given through the Spirit the message of wisdom, to another the message of knowledge by the same Spirit, to another faith,” etc. (1 Cor 12:8, 9). And though, as I have since seen, by this Scripture the Holy Spirit intends especially things extraordinary, yet on me it then fastened with conviction that I lacked things ordinary, even that understanding and wisdom that other Christians had. On this word I mused, and could not tell what to do; especially this word #emph[faith] pressed me hard, for I could not help it, but at times had to question whether I had any faith or not. For I feared that this lack shut me out of all the blessings that God had given other good people; but I was loath to conclude I had no faith in my soul; for if I do so, I thought, then I shall count myself a castaway indeed.

48\. No, I said to myself, though I am convinced that I am an ignorant dolt, and that I lack those blessed gifts of knowledge and understanding that other good people have, yet I will venture to conclude that I am not altogether faithless, though I do not know what faith is. For it was shown me, and that too, as I have seen since, by Satan, that those who conclude themselves to be in a faithless state have neither rest nor quiet in their souls; and I was loath to fall quite into despair.

49\. So, by this suggestion, I was for a while made afraid to see my lack of faith; but God would not allow me thus to undo and destroy my soul, but continually, against this blind and sad conclusion of mine, created within me such suppositions that I might be deceiving myself in this, that I could not rest content until I came to some certain knowledge whether I had faith or not; this always running in my mind: “But what if you lack faith indeed? But how can you tell you have faith?” And besides, I saw for certain that if I did not have it, I was sure to perish forever.

50\. So though I tried at first to pass over the business of faith, yet in a little time, considering the matter better, I was willing to put myself to the trial whether I had faith or not. But alas, poor wretch, so ignorant and brutish was I that at that time I knew no more how to do it than I know how to begin and accomplish that rare and intricate piece of art which I never yet saw or considered.

51\. So, while I was thus considering, and being put to my wits’ end about it (for you must know that as yet I had opened my mind in this matter to no man, but only heard and considered), the tempter came in with his delusion: that there was no way for me to know I had faith but by trying to work some miracle; urging those Scriptures that seem to look that way, to enforce and strengthen his temptation. Indeed, one day as I was between Elstow and Bedford, the temptation was hot upon me to try if I had faith by doing some miracle; and the miracle at that time was this: I must say to the puddles that were in the horse tracks, “Be dry,” and to the dry places, “Be puddles.” And truly, at one time I was going to say so indeed; but just as I was about to speak, this thought came into my mind: “But go under that hedge over there and pray first, that God would make you able.” But when I had decided to pray, this came hot upon me: that if I prayed, and came again and tried to do it, and yet did nothing for all that, then be sure I had no faith, but was a castaway and lost. No, I thought, if it is so, I will not try yet, but will wait a little longer.

52\. So I continued at a great loss; for I thought, if only those had faith who could do such wonderful things, then I concluded that for the present I neither had it, nor was ever likely to have it in time to come. Thus I was tossed between the devil and my own ignorance, and so perplexed, especially at times, that I could not tell what to do.

53\. About this time, the state and happiness of these poor people at Bedford was presented to me in this way, in a kind of #emph[vision]. I saw them as if they were set on the sunny side of some high mountain, there refreshing themselves with the pleasant beams of the sun, while I was shivering and shrinking in the cold, afflicted with frost, snow, and dark clouds. I thought, also, that between me and them I saw a wall that encircled this mountain; now, through this wall my soul greatly desired to pass, concluding that if I could, I would go even into the very midst of them, and there also comfort myself with the heat of their sun.

54\. I thought I went about this wall again and again, continually prying as I went, to see if I could find some way or passage by which I might enter in; but for some time I could find none. At last I saw, as it were, a narrow gap, like a little doorway in the wall, through which I attempted to pass. Now, the passage being very strait and narrow, I made many attempts to get in, but all in vain, until I was almost quite worn out by striving to get in. At last, with great striving, I thought I first got in my head, and after that, by a sidling striving, my shoulders, and my whole body; then I was exceedingly glad, and went and sat down in the midst of them, and so was comforted with the light and heat of their sun.

55\. Now, this mountain and wall, etc., was made out to me in this way: the mountain signified the church of the living God; the sun that shone on it, the comforting shining of His merciful face on those who were in it; the wall, I thought, was the Word, which made a separation between the Christians and the world; and the gap in this wall, I thought, was Jesus Christ, who is the way to God the Father (John 14:6; Matt 7:14). But since the passage was wonderfully narrow, so narrow that I could not enter in except with great difficulty, it showed me that none could enter into life but those who were in downright earnest, and unless they also left this wicked world behind them; for here there was room only for body and soul, but not for body and soul and sin.

56\. This figure stayed on my spirit many days. All that time I saw myself in a forlorn and sad condition, but yet I was stirred to a vehement hunger and desire to be one of that number who sat in the sunshine. Now also I would pray wherever I was, whether at home or away, in house or field, and would also often, with lifting up of heart, sing that line of the 51st Psalm, “O Lord, consider my distress”; for as yet I did not know where I was.

57\. Nor as yet could I attain to any comforting persuasion that I had faith in Christ; but instead of having satisfaction, I now began to find my soul assaulted with fresh doubts about my future happiness, especially with such as these: “Am I elected? But what if the day of grace should now be past and gone?”

58\. By these two temptations I was very much afflicted and disquieted, sometimes by one, and sometimes by the other of them. And first, to speak of my questioning my election: I found at this time that though I was in a flame to find the way to heaven and glory, and though nothing could beat me off from this, yet this question so troubled and discouraged me that I was, especially at some times, as if the very strength of my body had also been taken away by its force and power. This scripture also seemed to me to trample on all my desires: “It is not of him who wills, nor of him who runs, but of God who shows mercy” (Rom 9:16).

59\. With this scripture I could not tell what to do; for I clearly saw that unless the great God, of His infinite grace and bounty, had voluntarily chosen me to be a vessel of mercy, though I should desire, and long, and labor until my heart broke, no good could come of it. Therefore this question would always stick with me: “How can you tell you are elected? And what if you are not? What then?”

60\. “O Lord,” I thought, “what if I am not, indeed?” “It may be you are not,” said the tempter. “It may be so, indeed,” I thought. “Why, then,” said Satan, “you might as well leave off, and strive no further; for if indeed you should not be elected and chosen by God, there is no talk of your being saved; ‘for it is not of him who wills, nor of him who runs, but of God who shows mercy.’”

61\. By these things I was driven to my wits’ end, not knowing what to say, or how to answer these temptations. Indeed, I little thought that it was Satan who had assaulted me in this way; I thought rather that it was my own prudence to raise the question. For that the elect only attained eternal life, I heartily embraced without scruple; but whether I myself was one of them, there lay the question.

62\. So for several days I was greatly assaulted and perplexed, and often, when I was walking, I was ready to sink where I went, with faintness in my mind. But one day, after I had been oppressed and cast down by it for so many weeks, as I was now quite giving up the ghost of all my hopes of ever attaining life, that sentence fell with weight upon my spirit: “Look at the generations of old, and see: has anyone ever trusted in God and been confounded?”

63\. At this I was greatly lightened and encouraged in my soul; for at that very instant it was expounded to me in this way: “Begin at the beginning of Genesis, and read to the end of the Revelation, and see if you can find that there was anyone who ever trusted in the Lord and was confounded.” So, coming home, I went at once to my Bible to see if I could find that saying, not doubting that I would find it at once; for it was so fresh, and with such strength and comfort on my spirit, that it was as if it talked with me.

64\. Well, I looked, but I did not find it; only it stayed with me. Then I asked first this good man, and then another, if they knew where it was, but they knew of no such place. At this I wondered that such a sentence should so suddenly, and with such comfort and strength, seize and stay upon my heart, and yet that no one could find it; for I did not doubt that it was in Holy Scripture.

65\. So I continued above a year, and could not find the place; but at last, casting my eye into the books of the Apocrypha, I found it in Ecclesiasticus 2:10. At first this somewhat daunted me; but because by this time I had gained more experience of the love and kindness of God, it troubled me the less, especially when I considered that though it was not in those texts that we call holy and canonical, yet since this sentence was the sum and substance of many of the promises, it was my duty to take the comfort of it. And I bless God for that word, for it was of God to me: that word still, at times, shines before my face.

66\. After this, that other doubt came with strength upon me: “But what if the day of grace should be past and gone? What if you have overstayed the time of mercy?” Now, I remember that one day, as I was walking into the country, I was much in the thoughts of this: “But what if the day of grace is past?” And to aggravate my trouble, the tempter presented to my mind those good people of Bedford, and suggested this to me: that these, being converted already, were all that God would save in those parts; and that I came too late, for these had gotten the blessing before I came.

67\. Now I was in great distress, thinking in very deed that this might well be so. So I went up and down bemoaning my sad condition, counting myself far worse than a thousand fools for standing off so long, and spending so many years in sin as I had done, continually crying out, “Oh, that I had turned sooner! Oh, that I had turned seven years ago!” It also made me angry with myself to think that I should have had no more sense than to trifle away my time until my soul and heaven were lost.

68\. But when I had been long vexed with this fear, and was scarcely able to take one step more, just about the same place where I received my other encouragement, these words broke in upon my mind: “Compel them to come in, so that my house will be full”; “and still there is room” (Luke 14:22, 23). These words, but especially these, “And still there is room,” were sweet words to me; for truly I thought that by them I saw that there was place enough in heaven for me; and moreover, that when the Lord Jesus spoke these words, He was then thinking of me; and that He, knowing that the time would come when I would be afflicted with fear that there was no place left for me in His bosom, spoke this word beforehand and left it on record, so that I might find help by it against this vile temptation. This I then truly believed.

69\. In the light and encouragement of this word I went on for a good while; and the comfort was all the greater when I thought that the Lord Jesus should think of me so long ago, and that He should speak those words on purpose for my sake; for I truly thought that He spoke them on purpose to encourage me with them.

70\. But I was not without my temptations to go back again: temptations, I say, from Satan, from my own heart, and from carnal acquaintances. But I thank God these were outweighed by that sound sense of death and of the day of judgment, which stayed, as it were, continually in my view. I would also often think of Nebuchadnezzar, of whom it is said, “He had given him all the kingdoms of the earth” (Dan 5:19). “Yet,” I thought, “if this great man had all his portion in this world, one hour in hell fire would make him forget it all.” This consideration was a great help to me.

71\. About this time I was also made to see something concerning the beasts that Moses counted clean and unclean. I thought those beasts were types of men: the #emph[clean], types of those who were the people of God; but the #emph[unclean], types of such as were the children of the wicked one. Now, I read that the clean beasts “chewed the cud”; that is, I thought, they show us that we must feed upon the Word of God. They also parted “the hoof”; I thought that signified that we must part with the ways of ungodly men, if we would be saved. And also, in reading further about them, I found that though we chewed the cud as the #emph[hare] does, yet if we walked with claws like a dog, or if we parted the hoof like the swine, yet if we did not chew the cud as the sheep does, we were still, for all that, only unclean. For I thought the hare to be a type of those who talk of the Word, yet walk in ways of sin; and that the swine was like him who parts with his outward pollutions, but still lacks the Word of faith, without which there could be no way of salvation, however devout a man may be (Deut 14). After this I found, by reading the Word, that those who must be glorified with Christ in another world #emph[must be called] by Him here: called to share in His Word and righteousness, and in the comforts and firstfruits of His Spirit, and to a particular share in all those heavenly things which indeed fit the soul beforehand for that rest and house of glory which is in heaven above.

72\. Here again I was at a very great stand, not knowing what to do, fearing I was not called; for, I thought, if I am not called, what then can do me good? None but those who are effectually called inherit the kingdom of heaven. But oh, how I now loved those words that spoke of a #emph[Christian’s calling]! As when the Lord said to one, “Follow Me,” and to another, “Come after Me.” And oh, I thought, if only He would say so to me too, how gladly I would run after Him!

73\. I cannot now express with what longings and breathings in my soul I cried to Christ to call me. I continued like this for a time, all on a flame to be converted to Jesus Christ; and I also saw at that day such glory in a converted state that I could not be content without a share in it. Gold! If it could have been gotten for gold, what would I not have given for it! Had I had a whole world, it would all have gone ten thousand times over for this, that my soul might have been in a converted state.

74\. How lovely now was everyone in my eyes whom I thought to be converted men and women! They shone, they walked like a people who carried the broad seal of heaven about them. Oh, I saw the lot had fallen to them in pleasant places, and they had a goodly heritage (Ps 16:6). But what made me sick at heart was that saying of Christ in Mark: “He went up on the mountain and called for those He willed, and they came to Him” (Mark 3:13).

75\. This scripture made me faint and fear, yet it kindled fire in my soul. What made me fear was this: that Christ might have no liking for me, for He called “those He willed.” But oh, the glory that I saw in that condition still so engaged my heart that I could seldom read of anyone whom Christ called but I at once wished, “I wish I had been in their clothes! I wish I had been born Peter! I wish I had been born John!” or, “I wish I had been standing by and had heard Him when He called them: how I would have cried, ‘O Lord, call me also!’” But oh, I feared He would not call me.

76\. And truly the Lord let me go on like this for many months together, and showed me nothing, either that I was already called, or that I would be called hereafter. But at last, after much time spent, and many groans to God that I might be made a partaker of the holy and heavenly calling, that word came in upon me: “I will cleanse their blood, which I have not cleansed, for the #smallcaps[Lord] dwells in Zion” (Joel 3:21). These words, I thought, were sent to encourage me to wait still upon God, and signified to me that if I was not already truly converted to Christ, yet the time might come when I would be.

77\. About this time I began to open my mind to those poor people in Bedford, and to tell them my condition. When they had heard it, they told Mr. Gifford about me, and he himself also took occasion to talk with me, and was willing to think well of me, though I think on but little grounds. He invited me to his house, where I would hear him talk with others about the dealings of God with their souls; from all of which I received more and more conviction, and from that time began to see something of the vanity and inward wretchedness of my wicked heart, for as yet I knew no great matter of it. But now it began to be revealed to me, and also to work to a degree it never had before. Now I clearly found that lusts and corruptions put themselves forth within me, in wicked thoughts and desires, which I had not noticed before; my desires for heaven and life also began to fail. I found also that whereas before my soul was full of longings after God, now it began to hanker after every foolish vanity. Indeed, my heart would not be moved to attend to what was good; it began to be careless both of my soul and of heaven; it would now continually hang back, both from going to every duty and in it; and it was like a clog on the leg of a bird, to hinder me from flying.

78\. “No,” I thought, “now I grow worse and worse; now I am farther from conversion than I ever was before.” So I began to sink greatly in my soul, and began to entertain such discouragement in my heart as laid me as low as hell. If I were now to have been burned at the stake, I could not believe that Christ had love for me. Alas, I could neither hear Him, nor see Him, nor feel Him, nor savor any of His things. I was driven as with a tempest; my heart would be unclean; the Canaanites would dwell in the land.

79\. Sometimes I would tell my condition to the people of God, and when they heard it, they would pity me, and would tell me of the promises. But they might as well have told me that I must reach the sun with my finger as have bidden me receive or rely on the promise; and as soon as I tried to do it, all my sense and feeling were against me, and I saw I had a heart that would sin, and that lay under a law that would condemn.

80\. These things have often made me think of the child whom the father brought to Christ, who, while he was still coming to Him, was thrown down by the devil, and also so rent and torn by him that he lay and wallowed, foaming (Luke 9:42; Mark 9:20).

81\. Further, in these days I would find my heart shut itself up against the Lord and against His holy Word. I have found my unbelief set, as it were, its shoulder to the door to keep Him out, and that even when I have cried with many a bitter sigh, “Good Lord, break it open! Lord, break these gates of bronze, and cut through the bars of iron!” (Ps 107:16). Yet that word would sometimes create a peaceful pause in my heart: “I girded you, though you have not known Me” (Isa 45:5).

82\. But all this while, as to the act of sinning, I was never more tender than now. My hinder parts were inward; I dared not take a pin or a stick, even one no bigger than a straw, for my conscience was now sore, and would smart at every touch. I could not now tell how to speak my words, for fear I should misplace them. Oh, how gingerly I then went in all I did or said! I found myself as on a miry bog that shook if I did but stir, and felt as if I were left there by God and Christ and the Spirit and all good things.

83\. But I observe that though I was such a great sinner before conversion, God never much charged the guilt of the sins of my ignorance upon me; He only showed me that I was lost if I did not have Christ, because I had been a sinner. I saw that I lacked a perfect righteousness to present me without fault before God, and that this righteousness was nowhere to be found but in the person of Jesus Christ.

84\. But my original and inward pollution, that, that was my plague and my affliction. That I saw, at a dreadful rate, always putting itself forth within me; that I had the guilt of, to amazement; because of that I was more loathsome in my own eyes than a toad, and I thought I was so in God’s eyes too. Sin and corruption, I said, would bubble out of my heart as naturally as water bubbles out of a fountain. I thought now that everyone had a better heart than I had; I could have exchanged hearts with anybody; I thought none but the devil himself could equal me for inward wickedness and pollution of mind. So at the sight of my own vileness I fell deeply into despair; for I concluded that this condition I was in could not stand with a state of grace. “Surely,” I thought, “I am forsaken by God; surely I am given up to the devil, and to a reprobate mind.” And so I continued a long while, even for some years together.

85\. While I was so afflicted with the fears of my own damnation, there were two things that would make me wonder. The one was when I saw old people hunting after the things of this life, as if they would live here always; the other was when I found professing Christians much distressed and cast down when they met with outward losses, as of husband, wife, child, etc. “Lord,” I thought, “what a fuss is made here about such little things as these! What seeking after carnal things by some, and what grief in others for the loss of them! If they labor so much after the things of this present life, and spend so many tears for them, how am I to be bemoaned, pitied, and prayed for! My soul is dying, my soul is damning. If only my soul were in a good condition, and if only I were sure of it, ah, how rich I would count myself, though blessed with nothing but bread and water! I would count those things only small afflictions, and would bear them as little burdens. ‘But a wounded spirit, who can bear?’”

86\. And though I was so troubled, and tossed, and afflicted with the sight and sense and terror of my own wickedness, yet I was afraid to let this sense and sight go quite off my mind; for I found that unless guilt of conscience was taken off the right way, that is, by the blood of Christ, a man grew rather worse for the loss of his trouble of mind than better. So if my guilt lay hard upon me, then I would cry that the blood of Christ might take it off; and if it was going off without it (for the sense of sin would sometimes be as if it would die and go quite away), then I would also strive to fetch it back upon my heart again, by bringing the punishment for sin in hell fire upon my spirit; and I would cry, “Lord, do not let it go off my heart except the right way, by the blood of Christ, and by the application of Your mercy, through Him, to my soul.” For that scripture lay heavily upon me: “Without the shedding of blood there is no forgiveness” (Heb 9:22). And what made me the more afraid of this was that I had seen some who, when they were under wounds of conscience, would cry and pray; yet, seeking present ease from their trouble rather than pardon for their sin, they did not care how they lost their guilt, so long as they got it out of their mind. Now, having got it off the wrong way, it was not sanctified to them; but they grew harder and blinder, and more wicked after their trouble. This made me afraid, and made me cry to God the more that it might not be so with me.

87\. And now I was sorry that God had made me a man, for I feared I was a reprobate. I counted man, as unconverted, the most miserable of all the creatures. Being so afflicted and tossed about my sad condition, I counted myself alone, and more unblessed than most men.

88\. #emph[Indeed], I thought it impossible that I should ever attain to so much goodness of heart as to thank God that He had made me a man. Man indeed is the most noble of all creatures in the visible world by creation; but by sin he had made himself the most ignoble. The beasts, birds, fishes, etc.: I counted their condition blessed, for they did not have a sinful nature; they were not liable to the wrath of God; they were not to go to hell fire after death. So I could have rejoiced had my condition been as any of theirs.

89\. In this condition I went on a great while; but when comforting time had come, I heard one preach a sermon on these words in Song 4:1: “How beautiful you are, my love—how very beautiful!” But at that time he made these two words, “My love,” his chief subject matter; from which, after he had opened the text a little, he drew these several conclusions: 1. That the church, and so every saved soul, is Christ’s love, when loveless. 2. Christ’s love without a cause. 3. Christ’s love when hated by the world. 4. Christ’s love when under temptation, and under desertion. 5. Christ’s love from first to last.

90\. But I gained nothing from what he said at the time, except that when he came to the application of the fourth particular, this was the word he said: “If it is so, that the saved soul is Christ’s love when under temptation and desertion, then, poor tempted soul, when you are assaulted and afflicted with temptations, and the hidings of God’s face, yet always think on these two words, ‘My love.’”

91\. So as I was going home, these words came again into my thoughts; and I well remember that as they came in, I said this in my heart: “What shall I get by thinking on these two words?” This thought had no sooner passed through my heart than these words began to kindle in my spirit: “You are my love, you are my dove,” twenty times together. And continually, as they ran this way in my mind, they grew stronger and warmer, and began to make me look up; but being as yet between hope and fear, I still replied in my heart, “But is it true? But is it true?” At which that sentence fell upon me, that he “did not know that what was done by the angel was true” (Acts 12:9).

92\. Then I began to give place to the word, which with power, over and over, made this joyful sound within my soul: “You are my love, you are my love; and nothing shall separate you from my love”; and with that, Rom 8:39 came into my mind. Now my heart was filled full of comfort and hope, and now I could believe that my sins would be forgiven me. Indeed, I was now so taken with the love and mercy of God that I remember I did not know how to contain myself until I got home. I thought I could have spoken of His love, and told of His mercy to me, even to the very crows that sat on the plowed lands before me, had they been able to understand me. So I said in my soul with much gladness, “Well, I wish I had a pen and ink here; I would write this down before I go any further, for surely I will not forget this forty years from now.” But alas, within less than forty days I began to question everything again; and at times I fell back into my old ways again, which made me begin to question everything still more.

93\. Yet still, at times, I was helped to believe that it was a true manifestation of grace to my soul, though I had lost much of its life and savor. Now about a week or a fortnight after this, I was much followed by this scripture: “Simon, Simon, behold, Satan has desired to have you#super[+]” (Luke 22:31). And sometimes it would sound so loud within me, and, as it were, call so strongly after me, that once above all the rest I turned my head over my shoulder, truly thinking that some man behind me had called me, though he was at a great distance; he called so #emph[loud], I thought. It came, as I have thought since, to stir me up to prayer and to watchfulness; it came to let me know that a cloud and a storm were coming down upon me; but I did not understand it.

94\. Also, as I remember, #emph[that] time that it called to me so loud was the #emph[last] time that it sounded in my ears; but I think I still hear with what a loud voice these words, “Simon, Simon,” sounded in my ears. I truly thought, as I have told you, that somebody half a mile behind me had called after me; and although that was not my name, yet it made me suddenly look behind me, believing that he who called so loud meant me.

95\. But I was so foolish and ignorant that I did not know the reason for this sound, which, as I both saw and felt soon after, was sent from heaven as an alarm, to awaken me to provide for what was coming. Only I would muse and wonder in my mind, to think what could be the reason that this scripture, and that at this rate, so often and so loud, should continually be sounding and rattling in my ears. But, as I said before, I soon after perceived God’s purpose in it.

96\. For about the space of a month after, a very great storm came down upon me, which handled me twenty times worse than all I had met with before. It came stealing upon me, now by one piece, then by another. First, all my comfort was taken from me; then darkness seized upon me; after which whole floods of blasphemies, against God, Christ, and the Scriptures, were poured upon my spirit, to my great confusion and astonishment. These blasphemous thoughts were such as stirred up questions in me against the very #emph[being] of God, and of His only beloved Son: whether there were, in truth, a God, or a Christ? And whether the holy Scriptures were not rather a fable and a cunning story than the holy and pure Word of God?

97\. The tempter would also assault me much with this: “How can you tell but that the Turks had as good Scriptures to prove their Muhammad the Savior as we have to prove our Jesus is? And could I think that so many ten thousands, in so many countries and kingdoms, should be without the knowledge of the right way to heaven (if there were indeed a heaven), and that we only, who live in a corner of the earth, should alone be blessed with it? Everyone thinks his own religion the rightest, both Jews and Moors and pagans! And what if all our faith, and Christ, and Scriptures, should be only a think-so too?”

98\. Sometimes I have tried to argue against these suggestions, and to set some of the sentences of blessed Paul against them; but alas, when I did so, I quickly felt such arguings as these return upon me: “Though we make so great a matter of Paul and of his words, yet how could I tell but that in very deed he, being a subtle and cunning man, might give himself up to deceive with strong delusions, and also take the pains and labor to undo and destroy his fellows?”

99\. These suggestions, with many others which at this time I may not and dare not utter, either by word or by pen, made such a seizure upon my spirit, and so overweighed my heart, with their number, their continuance, and their fiery force, that I felt as if there were nothing else but these within me from morning to night, and as though indeed there could be room for nothing else. I also concluded that God had, in very wrath to my soul, given me up to them, to be carried away with them as with a mighty whirlwind.

100\. Only by the distaste that they gave to my spirit, I #emph[felt there was something in me that refused to embrace them]. But this consideration I had only when God gave me leave to swallow my spittle; otherwise the noise, and strength, and force of these temptations would drown and overflow, and as it were bury, all such thoughts, or the remembrance of any such thing. While I was in this temptation, I would often find my mind suddenly driven to curse and swear, or to speak some grievous thing against God, or Christ His Son, and against the Scriptures.

101\. Now I thought, “Surely I am possessed by the devil”; at other times again, I thought I would be bereft of my wits. For instead of praising and magnifying God the Lord with others, if I but heard Him spoken of, at once some most horrible blasphemous thought or other would bolt out of my heart against Him; so that whether I thought that God was, or again thought there was no such thing, I could feel no love, nor peace, nor gracious disposition within me.

102\. These things sank me into very deep despair; for I concluded that such things could not possibly be found among those who loved God. When these temptations were upon me with force, I often compared myself to the case of a child whom some gypsy has taken up by force in her arms, and is carrying away from friends and country. Kick I sometimes did, and also shriek and cry; but yet I was as if bound in the wings of the temptation, and the wind would carry me away. I thought also of Saul, and of the evil spirit that possessed him, and greatly feared that my condition was the same as his (1 Sam 16:14).

103\. In these days, when I heard others talk of what the sin against the Holy Spirit was, then the tempter would so provoke me to desire to sin that sin that I was as if I could not, must not, and would not be quiet until I had committed it. Now no sin would serve but that. If it were to be committed by speaking such a word, then I have been as if my mouth would have spoken that word, whether I wanted to or not; and so strongly was this temptation upon me that often I have been ready to clap my hand under my chin, to hold my mouth from opening; and to that end I have also had thoughts at other times of leaping headfirst into some dunghill hole or other, to keep my mouth from speaking.

104\. Now again I looked at the condition of the dog and the toad, and counted the state of everything that God had made far better than this dreadful state of mine, and than such as my companions’ was. Indeed, I would gladly have been in the condition of a dog or a horse, for I knew they had no soul to perish under the everlasting weight of hell, or of sin, as mine was likely to do. No, and though I saw this, felt this, and was broken to pieces with it, yet what added to my sorrow was that I could not find that I desired deliverance with all my soul. That scripture also tore and rent my soul in the midst of these distractions: “The wicked are like the storm-tossed sea, for it cannot be still, and its waves churn up mire and muck. ‘There is no peace,’ says my God, ‘for the wicked.’”

105\. And now my heart was at times exceedingly hard. If I would have given a thousand pounds for a tear, I could not shed one; no, nor sometimes scarcely desire to shed one. I was much dejected to think that this would be my lot. I saw some could mourn and lament their sin; and others, again, could rejoice and bless God for Christ; and others, again, could quietly talk of the Word of God, and remember it with gladness; while I alone was in the storm or tempest. This sank me much; I thought my condition was alone. So I would much bewail my hard lot; but get out of these things, or get rid of them, I could not.

106\. While this temptation lasted, which was about a year, I could attend none of the ordinances of God but with sore and great affliction. Indeed, I was then most distressed with blasphemies. If I was hearing the Word, then uncleanness, blasphemies, and despair would hold me captive there; if I was reading, then sometimes I had sudden thoughts to question all I read; sometimes, again, my mind would be so strangely snatched away, and possessed with other things, that I neither knew, nor regarded, nor remembered so much as the sentence that I had just now read.

107\. In prayer also I was greatly troubled at this time. Sometimes I thought I would see the devil; indeed, I thought I felt him behind me, pulling my clothes. He would also be continually at me in time of prayer to have done: “Break off, make haste, you have prayed enough, and stay no longer,” continually drawing my mind away. Sometimes also he would cast in such wicked thoughts as these: that I must pray to him, or for him. I have sometimes thought of that: “Fall down,” or, “if You will fall down and worship me” (Matt 4:9).

108\. Also, when, because I had wandering thoughts in the time of this duty, I have labored to compose my mind and fix it upon God, then with great force the tempter has labored to distract me and confound me, and to turn away my mind, by presenting to my heart and imagination the form of a bush, a bull, a broom, or the like, as if I should pray to those. To these also he would, at some times especially, so hold my mind that I was as if I could think of nothing else, or pray to nothing else, but these, or such as they.

109\. #emph[Yet] at times I would have some strong and heart-affecting apprehensions of God, and of the reality of the truth of His gospel; but oh, how my heart would at such times put itself forth with inexpressible groanings! My whole soul was then in every word; I would cry with pangs after God that He would be merciful to me. But then I would be daunted again with such fancies as these: I would think that God mocked at these prayers of mine, saying, and that in the hearing of the holy angels, “This poor simple wretch hankers after Me as if I had nothing to do with My mercy but to bestow it on such as he. Alas, poor soul! How you are deceived! It is not for such as you to have favor with the Highest.”

110\. Then the tempter has also come upon me with such discouragements as these: “You are very hot for mercy, but I will cool you; this frame will not last always; many have been as hot as you for a spurt, but I have quenched their zeal.” And with this, such and such who had fallen away would be set before my eyes. Then I would be afraid that I would do so too; but, I thought, “I am glad this comes into my mind. Well, I will watch, and take what heed I can.” “Though you do,” said Satan, “I shall be too hard for you; I will cool you imperceptibly, by degrees, little by little. What do I care,” he says, “though I am seven years chilling your heart, if I can do it at last? Continual rocking will lull a crying child to sleep. I will ply it close, but I will have my end accomplished. Though you are burning hot at present, yet if I can pull you from this fire, I shall have you cold before long.”

111\. These things brought me into great straits; for as I could not at present find myself fit for present death, so I thought that to live long would make me yet more unfit; for time would make me forget everything, and wear even the remembrance of the evil of sin, the worth of heaven, and the need I had of the blood of Christ to wash me, out of both mind and thought. But I thank Christ Jesus these things did not at present make me slack in my crying, but rather put me more upon it, like the woman who met with the adulterer (Deut 22:27). In those days that was a good word to me, after I had suffered these things a while: “I am convinced that neither height nor depth, nor death nor life,” etc., “will be able to separate us from the love of God that is in Christ Jesus” (Rom 8:38). And now I hoped long life would not destroy me, nor make me miss heaven.

112\. Yet I had some supports in this temptation, though they were then all questioned by me. That in the third of Jeremiah, at the first, was something to me, and so was the consideration of the fifth verse of that chapter: that though we have spoken and done as evil things as we could, yet we should cry to God, “My Father, You are my friend from youth,” and should return to Him.

113\. I had, also, once a sweet glance from that in 2 Corinthians 5:21: “God made Him who knew no sin to be sin on our behalf, so that in Him we might become the righteousness of God.” I remember that one day, as I was sitting in a neighbor’s house, very sad there at the consideration of my many blasphemies, and as I was saying in my mind, “What ground do I have to think that I, who have been so vile and abominable, should ever inherit eternal life?” that word came suddenly upon me: “What then shall we say in response to these things? If God is for us, who can be against us?” (Rom 8:31). That also was a help to me: “Because I live, you#super[+] also will live” (John 14:19). But these words were only hints, touches, and short visits, though very sweet when present; only they did not last, but, like Peter’s sheet, they were suddenly caught up from me to heaven again (Acts 10:16).

114\. But afterward the Lord revealed Himself to me more fully and graciously; and indeed He completely delivered me, not only from the guilt that these things had laid upon my conscience, but also from their very filth; for the temptation was removed, and I was put into my right mind again, as other Christians were.

115\. I remember that one day, as I was traveling into the country and musing on the wickedness and blasphemy of my heart, and considering the enmity that was in me against God, that scripture came into my mind, He has “made peace through the blood of His cross” (Col 1:20). By this I was made to see, again and again that day, that God and my soul were friends by this blood; yes, I saw that the justice of God and my sinful soul could embrace and kiss each other through this blood. This was a good day to me; I hope I shall not forget it.

116\. At another time, as I sat by the fire in my house, musing on my wretchedness, the Lord made this also a precious word to me: “Now since the children have flesh and blood, He too shared in their humanity, so that by His death He might destroy him who holds the power of death, that is, the devil, and free those who all their lives were held in slavery by their fear of death” (Heb 2:14, 15). I thought that the glory of those words was then so weighty on me that I was, once and twice, ready to faint as I sat; yet not with grief and trouble, but with solid joy and peace.

117\. At this time, also, I sat under the ministry of holy Mr. Gifford, whose doctrine, by God’s grace, did much for my stability. This man made it much his business to deliver the people of God from all those false and unsound rests that by nature we are prone to take and make for our souls. He would bid us take special care that we did not take up any truth on trust, as from this or that or any other man or men, but cry mightily to God that He would convince us of the reality of it, and settle us in it by His own Spirit in the holy Word. “For,” he said, “if you#super[+] do otherwise, when temptations come, if they come strongly, you, not having received these truths with evidence from heaven, will find that you lack the help and strength to resist that you once thought you had.”

118\. This was as timely to my soul as the former and latter rain in their season; for I had found, and that by sad experience, the truth of these words of his. For I had felt that “no man can #emph[say],” especially when tempted by the devil, “that Jesus Christ is Lord except by the Holy Spirit.” So I found my soul, through grace, very ready to drink in this doctrine, and inclined to pray to God that, in nothing that concerned God’s glory and my own eternal happiness, He would allow me to be without its confirmation from heaven. For now I saw clearly that there was an exceedingly great difference between the notions of flesh and blood and the revelations of God in heaven; and also a great difference between the faith that is feigned, and according to man’s wisdom, and the faith that comes by a man’s being born to it of God (Matt 16:15–17; 1 John 5:1).

119\. But oh, how my soul was now led from truth to truth by God! even from the birth and cradle of the Son of God to His ascension and His second coming from heaven to judge the world.

120\. Truly, I then found, on this account, that the great God was very good to me; for, as far as I remember, there was not anything that I then cried to God to make known and reveal to me but He was pleased to do it for me; I mean that there was not one part of the gospel of the Lord Jesus but I was led into it in order. It seemed to me that I saw with great evidence, from the account of the four evangelists, the wonderful work of God in giving Jesus Christ to save us, from His conception and birth even to His second coming to judgment. It was as if I had seen Him born, as if I had seen Him grow up, as if I had seen Him walk through this world, from the cradle to the cross: and when He came to the cross, I saw how gently He gave Himself to be hanged and nailed on it for my sins and wicked doings. Also, as I was musing on this progress of His, this dropped on my spirit: “He was ordained for the slaughter” (1 Pet 1:19, 20).

121\. When I have also considered the truth of His resurrection, and have remembered that word, “Do #emph[not] touch Me, Mary,” and so on, I have seen as if He had leaped out of the grave’s mouth for joy that He was risen again, and had gotten the victory over our dreadful foes (John 20:17). I have also, in the spirit, seen Him a man at the right hand of God the Father for me, and have seen the manner of His coming from heaven to judge the world with glory, and have been confirmed in these things by these scriptures following: Acts 1:9, 10; 7:56; 10:42; Heb 7:24; 8:3; Rev 1:18; 1 Thess 4:17, 18.

122\. Once I was troubled to know whether the Lord Jesus was man as well as God, and God as well as man; and truly, in those days, let men say what they would, unless I had it with evidence from heaven, it was all nothing to me; I did not count myself settled in any truth of God. Well, I was much troubled about this point, and could not tell how to be resolved. At last that verse in the fifth chapter of Revelation came into my mind: “And I looked, and behold, in the midst of the throne and of the four living creatures, and in the midst of the elders, stood a Lamb.” In the midst of the throne, I thought, there is His Godhead; in the midst of the elders, there is His manhood. Oh, how this seemed to me to glitter! It was a goodly touch, and gave me sweet satisfaction. That other scripture also helped me much in this: “Unto us a child is born, unto us a son is given, and the government will be upon His shoulders. And He will be called Wonderful Counselor, Mighty God, Everlasting Father, Prince of Peace,” and so on (Isa 9:6).

123\. Also, besides these teachings of God in His Word, the Lord made use of two things to confirm me in this truth: the one was the errors of the Quakers, and the other was the guilt of sin. For as the Quakers opposed this truth, so God confirmed me in it all the more, by leading me into the scriptures that wonderfully maintain it.

124\. The errors that this people then maintained were:

1\. That the holy Scriptures were not the Word of God.

2\. That every man in the world had the spirit of Christ, grace, faith, and so on.

3\. That Christ Jesus, as crucified, and dying sixteen hundred years ago, did not satisfy divine justice for the sins of His people.

4\. That Christ’s flesh and blood was within the saints.

5\. That the bodies of the good and bad that are buried in the churchyard shall not rise again.

6\. That the resurrection is already past with good men.

7\. That the man Jesus, who was crucified between two thieves on Mount Calvary, in the land of Canaan, near Jerusalem, had not ascended up above the starry heavens.

8\. That He, the very same Jesus who died by the hand of the Jews, would not come again at the last day, and as man judge all nations, and so on.

125\. Many more vile and abominable things were stirred up by them in those days, by which I was driven to a closer search of the Scripture, and was, through its light and testimony, not only enlightened but greatly confirmed and comforted in the truth. And, as I said, the guilt of sin helped me much; for whenever that came upon me, the blood of Christ took it off again, and again, and again, and that sweetly too, according to the Scriptures. O friends, cry to God to reveal Jesus Christ to you#super[+]; there is none who teaches like Him.

126\. It would take too long to stay here and tell you in detail how God settled me in all the things of Christ, and how, so that He might do so, He led me into His words; yes, and also how He opened them to me, and made them shine before me, and caused them to dwell with me, talk with me, and comfort me over and over, concerning both His own being and the being of His Son, and Spirit, and Word, and gospel.

127\. Only this, as I said before, I will say to you again: in general He was pleased to take this course with me. First He allowed me to be afflicted with temptation concerning these things, and then He revealed them to me. For example, at times I would lie under great guilt for sin, even crushed to the ground with it, and then the Lord would show me the death of Christ; yes, and so sprinkle my conscience with His blood that I would find, and that before I was aware, that in the very conscience where just now the law reigned and raged, there the peace and love of God through Christ would rest and abide.

128\. Now I had an evidence, as I thought, of my salvation from heaven, with many golden seals on it, all hanging in my sight. Now I could remember this manifestation and that other revelation of grace with comfort, and would often long and desire that the last day had come, that I might be forever inflamed with the sight and joy and communion of Him whose head was crowned with thorns, whose face was spit on, and body broken, and soul made an offering for my sins. For whereas before I lay continually trembling at the mouth of hell, now it seemed to me I had got so far from it that, when I looked back, I could scarcely make it out. And oh, I thought, if only I were eighty years old now, that I might die quickly, that my soul might be gone to rest.

129\. But before I had got this far out of these temptations of mine, I greatly longed to see the experience of some ancient godly man who had written some hundreds of years before I was born. For as for those who had written in our days, I thought (but I ask them now to pardon me) that they had written only what others felt, or else had, through the strength of their wits and abilities, studied to answer such objections as they saw others were perplexed with, without going down into the deep themselves. Well, after many such longings in my mind, the God in whose hand are all our days and ways cast into my hand one day a book of Martin Luther’s; it was his commentary on Galatians, and it was so old that it was ready to fall apart piece by piece if I did but turn it over. Now I was much pleased that such an old book had fallen into my hand; and when I had read only a little way in it, I found my condition so largely and profoundly handled in his experience that it was as if his book had been written out of my heart. This made me marvel; for this is what I thought: “This man could not know anything of the state of Christians now, but must surely write and speak the experience of former days.”

130\. Besides, in that book he also debates most gravely the rise of these temptations, namely, blasphemy, despair, and the like, showing that the law of Moses, as well as the devil, death, and hell, has a very great hand in them. At first this was very strange to me; but by considering and watching, I found it so indeed. But I intend nothing of particulars here; only this, I think, I must declare before all men: I prefer this book of Martin Luther upon Galatians, except the Holy Bible, before all the books that I have ever seen, as most fit for a wounded conscience.

131\. And now I found, as I thought, that I loved Christ dearly. Oh, it seemed to me that my soul clung to Him, my affections clung to Him; I felt love to Him as hot as fire. And now, as Job said, I thought I should die in my nest. But I quickly found that my great love was only little, and that I, who had, as I thought, such burning love to Jesus Christ, could let Him go again for a very trifle. God knows how to humble us, and can hide pride from man. Soon after this my love was tried in earnest.

132\. For after the Lord had in this manner so graciously delivered me from this great and severe temptation, and had settled me so sweetly in the faith of His holy gospel, and had given me such strong consolation and blessed evidence from heaven concerning my interest in His love through Christ, the tempter came upon me again, and that with a more grievous and dreadful temptation than before.

133\. And that was, to “sell and part with this most blessed Christ, to exchange Him for the things of this life, for anything.” The temptation lay upon me for the space of a year, and followed me so continually that I was not free of it one day in a month; no, at times not for one hour in many days together, unless I was asleep.

134\. And though in my judgment I was persuaded that those who were once effectually in Christ (as I hoped, through His grace, I had seen myself to be) could never lose Him forever (“For the land must not be sold forever, for the land is Mine,” says God, Lev 25:23), yet it was a continual vexation to me to think that I should have so much as one such thought within me against a Christ, a Jesus, who had done for me as He had done; and yet at that time I had almost no other thoughts but such blasphemous ones.

135\. But neither my dislike of the thought, nor any desire and endeavor to resist it, in the least shook or lessened its continuance, or its force and strength. For it always mixed itself in with almost whatever I thought, in such a way that I could neither eat my food, stoop for a pin, chop a stick, nor cast my eye to look on this or that, but the temptation would always come: “Sell Christ for this, or sell Christ for that; sell Him, sell Him.”

136\. Sometimes it would run in my thoughts no fewer than a hundred times together, “Sell Him, sell Him, sell Him.” Against this, I may say, for whole hours together I have been forced to stand as if continually leaning and forcing my spirit against it, lest perhaps, before I was aware, some wicked thought might arise in my heart that might consent to it. And sometimes also the tempter would make me believe I had consented to it, and then I would be as if tortured on a rack for whole days together.

137\. This temptation put me in such fright, lest at some time, I say, I should consent to it and be overcome by it, that by the very force of my mind in laboring to contradict and resist this wickedness, my very body would be put into action or motion, by way of pushing or thrusting with my hands or elbows, all the while answering as fast as the destroyer said, “Sell Him”: “I will not, I will not, I will not, I will not; no, not for thousands, thousands, thousands of worlds.” I reckoned so, lest in the midst of these assaults I should set too low a value on Him, even until I scarcely knew where I was, or how to be composed again.

138\. At these seasons he would not let me eat my food in quiet; but, of all things, when I was set at the table at my meal, I must go away from it to pray; I must leave my food now, just now, so counterfeit-holy would this devil be too. When I was tempted in this way, I would say within myself, “Now I am at my meal; let me make an end.” “No,” he said, “you must do it now, or you will displease God and despise Christ.” So I was much afflicted with these things; and because of the sinfulness of my nature, imagining that these things were impulses from God, I would refuse to do it, as if I were denying God; and then I would be as guilty, because I did not obey a temptation of the devil, as if I had broken the law of God indeed.

139\. But to be brief: one morning, as I lay in my bed, I was, as at other times, most fiercely assaulted with this temptation, to “sell and part with Christ”; the wicked suggestion kept running in my mind, “Sell Him, sell Him, sell Him, sell Him, sell Him,” as fast as a man could speak. Against it also, in my mind, as at other times, I answered, “No, no, not for thousands, thousands, thousands,” at least twenty times together. But at last, after much striving, even until I was almost out of breath, I felt this thought pass through my heart, “Let Him go, if He #emph[will]!” and I thought also that I felt my heart freely consent to it. Oh, the diligence of Satan! Oh, the desperateness of man’s heart!

140\. Now the battle was won, and down I fell, as a bird that is shot from the top of a tree, into great guilt and fearful despair. So, getting out of my bed, I went moping into the field; but God knows, with as heavy a heart as mortal man, I think, could bear. There, for the space of two hours, I was like a man bereft of life, and as now past all recovery, and bound over to eternal punishment.

141\. And with that, this scripture seized upon my soul: “Or profane person, like Esau, who for one morsel of food sold his birthright. For you#super[+] know that afterward, when he wanted to inherit the blessing, he was rejected; for he found no place of repentance, though he sought it carefully with tears” (Heb 12:16, 17).

142\. Now I was as one bound; I felt myself shut up to the judgment to come. For two years together nothing would now abide with me but damnation, and an expectation of damnation; I say, nothing would now abide with me but this, except some few moments of relief, as you will see in what follows.

143\. These words were to my soul like fetters of brass on my legs, and I went about in their continual sound for several months together. But about ten or eleven o’clock on that same day, as I was walking under a hedge, full of sorrow and guilt, God knows, and bemoaning myself for this hard lot, that such a thought should arise within me, suddenly this sentence bolted in on me: “The blood of Christ remits all guilt.” At this I made a stand in my spirit; and with that, this word took hold upon me: “The blood of Jesus Christ, His Son, cleanses us from all sin” (1 John 1:7).

144\. Now I began to conceive peace in my soul, and it seemed to me as if I saw the tempter leer and steal away from me, as being ashamed of what he had done. At the same time also I had my sin and the blood of Christ set before me in this way: that my sin, when compared to the blood of Christ, was no more to it than this little clod or stone before me is to this vast and wide field that I see here. This gave me good encouragement for the space of two or three hours; in which time also, it seemed to me, I saw by faith the Son of God suffering for my sins. But because it did not stay, I sank in my spirit under exceedingly great guilt again.

145\. But this was chiefly because of the scripture I have mentioned, concerning Esau’s selling of his birthright; for that scripture would lie all day long, all the week long, yes, all the year long in my mind, and hold me down, so that I could by no means lift myself up. For when I would strive to turn to this scripture or that for relief, that sentence would always be sounding in me: “For you#super[+] know that afterward, when he wanted to inherit the blessing—he found no place of repentance, though he sought it carefully with tears.”

146\. Sometimes, indeed, I would have a touch from that word in Luke 22:32, “I have prayed for you, that your faith will not fail”; but it would not abide upon me. Nor indeed could I, when I considered my state, find ground to conceive in the least that the root of that grace should be within me, having sinned as I had done. Now I was torn and rent in a heavy case, for many days together.

147\. Then I began, with a sad and anxious heart, to consider the nature and largeness of my sin, and to search in the Word of God whether I could find in any place a word of promise, or any encouraging sentence, from which I might take relief. So I began to consider that word in Mark: “The sons of men will be forgiven all sins and blasphemies, as many as they utter.” This place, it seemed to me at first sight, contained a large and glorious promise for the pardon of great offenses; but considering the place more fully, I thought it was rather to be understood as relating chiefly to those who, while in a natural state, had committed such things as are mentioned there; but not to me, who had not only received light and mercy, but had, both after it and also contrary to it, slighted Christ as I had done.

148\. I feared therefore that this wicked sin of mine might be that unpardonable sin of which He speaks there: “But whoever blasphemes against the Holy Spirit never has forgiveness, but is in danger of eternal damnation” (Mark 3:29). And I gave all the more credit to this because of that sentence in Hebrews: “For you#super[+] know that afterward, when he wanted to inherit the blessing, he was rejected; for he found no place of repentance, though he sought it carefully with tears.” And this stuck with me always.

149\. And now I was both a burden and a terror to myself; nor did I ever know so well as now what it was to be weary of my life, and yet afraid to die. Oh, how gladly now would I have been anybody but myself! Anything but a man! and in any condition but my own! For nothing passed more frequently over my mind than that it was impossible for me to be forgiven my transgression, and to be saved from the wrath to come.

150\. And now I began to labor to call back the time that was past, wishing a thousand times twice told that the day were yet to come when I should be tempted to such a sin; concluding, with great indignation both against my heart and against all assaults, that I would rather have been torn in pieces than be found consenting to it. But alas! these thoughts and wishings and resolvings were now too late to help me; the thought had passed through my heart; God has let me go, and I have fallen. “Oh,” I thought, “that it were with me as in months past, as in the days when God preserved me!” (Job 29:2).

151\. Then again, being loath and unwilling to perish, I began to compare my sin with the sins of others, to see if I could find that any of those who are saved had done as I had done. So I considered David’s adultery and murder, and found them most heinous crimes, and those too committed after light and grace received. But yet, by considering, I perceived that his transgressions were only such as were against the law of Moses, from which the Lord Christ could, with the consent of His Word, deliver him; but mine was against the gospel, yes, against its Mediator; I had sold my Savior.

152\. Now again I would be as if racked upon the wheel, when I considered that, besides the guilt that possessed me, I should be so void of grace, so bewitched. What, I thought, must it be no sin but this? Must it necessarily be the “great transgression” (Ps 19:13)? Must #emph[that] wicked one touch my soul (1 John 5:18)? Oh, what stings I found in all these sentences!

153\. What, I thought, is there but one sin that is unpardonable? But #emph[one] sin that lays the soul beyond the reach of God’s mercy; and must I be guilty of #emph[that]? Must it necessarily be that one? Is there but one sin among so many millions of sins for which there is no forgiveness; and must I commit #emph[this]? Oh, unhappy sin! Oh, unhappy man! These things would so break and confound my spirit that I could not tell what to do; I thought at times they would have broken my wits. And always, to aggravate my misery, this would run in my mind: “You#super[+] know that afterward, when he wanted to inherit the blessing, he was rejected.” Oh, no one knows the terrors of those days but myself!

154\. After this I came to consider Peter’s sin, which he committed in denying his Master; and indeed, this came nearest to mine of any that I could find. For he had denied his Savior, as I had, after light and mercy received; yes, and that too after warning was given him. I also considered that he did it both once and twice, and that after he had time to consider in between. But though I put all these circumstances together, that if possible I might find help, yet I considered again that his was only a #emph[denial of his Master], but mine was a #emph[selling of my Savior]. So I thought to myself that I came nearer to Judas than to either David or Peter.

155\. Here again my torment would flame out and afflict me; yes, it would grind me, as it were, to powder, to consider God’s preservation of others while I fell into the snare. For in thus considering other men’s sins, and comparing them with my own, I could plainly see that God preserved them, notwithstanding their wickedness, and would not let them, as He had let me, become a son of perdition.

156\. But oh, how my soul at this time prized the preservation that God set about His people! Ah, how safely I saw them walk, whom God had hedged in! They were within His care, protection, and special providence; though by nature they were fully as bad as I, yet because He loved them, He would not allow them to fall outside the range of mercy. But as for me, I was gone, I had done it; He would not preserve me, nor keep me, but allowed me, because I was a reprobate, to fall as I had done. Now those blessed places that speak of God’s keeping His people shone like the sun before me, though not to comfort me, yet to show me the blessed state and heritage of those whom the Lord had blessed.

157\. Now I saw that, as God had His hand in all the providences and dispensations that overtook His elect, so He had His hand in all the temptations that they had to sin against Him; not to incite them to wickedness, but to choose their temptations and troubles for them, and also to leave them for a time to such sins only as might not destroy but humble them, as might not put them beyond, but lay them in the way of, the renewing of His mercy. But oh, what love, what care, what kindness and mercy I now saw mixing itself with the most severe and dreadful of all God’s ways with His people! He would let David, Hezekiah, Solomon, Peter, and others fall, but He would not let them fall into the unpardonable sin, nor into hell for sin. Oh, I thought, these are the men whom God has loved; these are the men whom God, though He chastens them, keeps in safety by Him, and whom He makes to abide under the shadow of the Almighty. But all these thoughts added sorrow, grief, and horror to me, since whatever I now thought on was killing to me. If I thought how God kept His own, that was killing to me. If I thought of how I had fallen myself, that was killing to me. As all things worked together for the best, and to do good to those who were the called according to His purpose, so I thought that all things worked for my harm, and for my eternal overthrow.

158\. Then again I began to compare my sin with the sin of Judas, that if possible I might find whether mine differed from that which in truth is unpardonable. And oh, I thought, if it should differ from it, though only by the breadth of a hair, what a happy condition my soul is in! And by considering, I found that Judas did his intentionally, but mine was against my prayer and strivings; besides, his was committed with much deliberation, but mine in a fearful hurry, on a sudden. All this while I was tossed to and fro like the locusts, and driven from trouble to sorrow, always hearing the sound of Esau’s fall in my ears, and of its dreadful consequences.

159\. Yet this consideration about Judas and his sin was, for a while, some little relief to me; for I saw that, as to the circumstances, I had not transgressed so foully as he. But this was quickly gone again; for I thought to myself, there might be more ways than one to commit the unpardonable sin; also I thought that there might be degrees of it, as well as of other transgressions. So, for all I could yet perceive, this iniquity of mine might be such as might never be passed by.

160\. I was often now ashamed that I should be like such an ugly man as Judas; I thought also how loathsome I would be to all the saints at the day of judgment; so much so that now I could scarcely see a good man, whom I believed to have a good conscience, but I would feel my heart tremble at him while I was in his presence. Oh, now I saw a glory in walking with God, and what a mercy it was to have a good conscience before Him.

161\. About this time I was much tempted to content myself by receiving some false opinion, such as that there should be no such thing as a day of judgment, that we should not rise again, and that sin was no such grievous thing. The tempter suggested this: “For even if these things should indeed be true, yet to believe otherwise would give you ease for the present. If you must perish, do not torment yourself so much beforehand; drive the thoughts of damnation out of your mind by filling your mind with some such conclusions as Atheists and Ranters use to help themselves.”

162\. But oh, when such thoughts have passed through my heart, how death and judgment have been in my view, as it were within a step! It seemed to me that the Judge stood at the door; it was as if it had come already; so that such things could find no welcome. But I think I see by this that Satan will use any means to keep the soul from Christ. He does not love an awakened frame of spirit; complacency, blindness, darkness, and error are the very kingdom and dwelling place of the wicked one.

163\. I found it hard work now to pray to God, because despair was swallowing me up. I thought I was driven away from God as with a tempest; for always when I cried to God for mercy, this would come in: “It is too late; I am lost; God has let me fall, not to my correction, but to condemnation; my sin is unpardonable; and I know concerning Esau how, after he had sold his birthright, he wanted to receive the blessing, but was rejected.” About this time I came upon that dreadful story of that miserable mortal, Francis Spira, a book that was to my troubled spirit as salt rubbed into a fresh wound. Every sentence in that book, every groan of that man, with all the rest of his actions in his anguish, as his tears, his prayers, his gnashing of teeth, his wringing of hands, his twining and twisting, and languishing and pining away under that mighty hand of God that was upon him, was as knives and daggers in my soul. Especially that sentence of his was frightful to me: “Man knows the beginning of sin, but who sets bounds to where it will end?” Then the former sentence, as the conclusion of all, would fall again upon my conscience like a hot thunderbolt: “for you#super[+] know that afterward, when he wanted to inherit the blessing, he was rejected; for he found no place of repentance, though he sought it carefully with tears.”

164\. Then I would be struck into a very great trembling, so much so that at times, for whole days together, I could feel my very body, as well as my mind, shake and totter under the sense of the dreadful judgment of God that should fall on those who have sinned that most fearful and unpardonable sin. I felt also such a clogging and heat at my stomach, because of this terror of mine, that, especially at some times, it was as if my breastbone would have split open. Then I thought of what is said of Judas, who, “falling headlong, burst open in the middle, and all his intestines spilled out” (Acts 1:18).

165\. I feared also that this was the mark that the Lord set on Cain, namely, continual fear and trembling under the heavy load of guilt that He had charged on him for the blood of his brother Abel. So I would wind, and twist, and shrink under the burden that was upon me; and this burden so oppressed me that I could neither stand, nor walk, nor lie down, either at rest or quiet.

166\. Yet that saying would sometimes come to mind, “He has received gifts for the rebellious” (Ps 68:18). “The #emph[rebellious],” I thought; why, surely they are those who were once in subjection to their prince, even those who, after they have sworn subjection to his government, have taken up arms against him. And this, I thought, is my very condition: I once loved Him, feared Him, served Him; but now I am a rebel; I have sold Him; I have said, “Let Him go if He #emph[will]”; but yet He has gifts for rebels, and then why not for me?

167\. I sometimes thought on this, and would labor to take hold of it, so that some refreshment, though small, might be conceived by me; but in this also I missed my desire. I was driven by force beyond it; I was like a man going to the place of execution, even past #emph[that] place where he would gladly creep in and hide himself, but may not.

168\. Again, after I had thus considered the sins of the saints in particular, and found that mine went beyond them, I began to think this way with myself: Suppose I should put #emph[all theirs] together, and #emph[mine alone] against them, might I not then find some encouragement? For if mine, though bigger than any one of theirs, should yet be only equal to all of them, then there is hope; for the blood that has virtue enough in it to wash away all theirs has virtue enough in it to do away mine, though this one were fully as big as all theirs, if not bigger. Here again I would consider the sin of David, of Solomon, of Manasseh, of Peter, and the rest of the great offenders; and I would also labor, as far as I fairly could, to aggravate and heighten their sins by various circumstances.

169\. I would think to myself that David shed blood to cover his adultery, and that by the sword of the children of Ammon, a work that could not be done except by continued, deliberate contrivance, which was a great aggravation of his sin. But then this would turn upon me: Ah! but these were only sins against the law, from which a Jesus was sent to save them; but yours is a sin against the Savior, and who shall save you from that?

170\. Then I thought of Solomon, and how he sinned in loving foreign women, in falling away to their idols, in building them temples, in doing this after light, in his old age, after great mercy received. But the same conclusion that cut me off in the former consideration cut me off as to this, namely, that all those were only sins against the law, for which God had provided a remedy; but I had #emph[sold my Savior], and there now remained no more sacrifice for sin.

171\. I would then add to these men’s sins the sins of Manasseh: how he built altars for idols in the house of the Lord; he also observed omens, used enchantments, had dealings with wizards, was a wizard, had his familiar spirits, burned his children in the fire in sacrifice to devils, and made the streets of Jerusalem run down with the blood of innocents. These, I thought, are great sins, sins of a bloody color; but yet it would turn upon me again: “None of them is of the nature of yours; you have parted with Jesus, you have sold your Savior.”

172\. This one consideration would always kill my heart: “My sin was point-blank against my Savior”; and that too to such a height that I had said of Him in my heart, “Let Him go if He will.” Oh, it seemed to me that this sin was bigger than the sins of a country, of a kingdom, or of the whole world; no one pardonable sin, nor all of them together, was able to equal mine; mine outwent every one of them.

173\. Now I would feel my mind flee from God, as from the face of a dreadful judge; yet this was my torment, that I could not escape His hand: “It is a fearful thing to fall into the hands of the living God” (Heb 10:31). But blessed be His grace, that scripture, in these fleeing fits, would call as if running after me: “I have blotted out your transgressions like a cloud, and your sins like a mist. Return to Me, for I have redeemed you” (Isa 44:22). This, I say, would come in upon my mind when I was fleeing from the face of God; for I did flee from His face, that is, my mind and spirit fled before Him; because of His highness I could not endure Him. Then the text would cry, “Return to Me”; it would cry aloud with a very great voice, “Return to Me, for I have redeemed you.” Indeed, this would make me stop a little, and, as it were, look over my shoulder behind me, to see whether I could discern that the God of grace was following me with a pardon in His hand. But no sooner could I do that than all would be clouded and darkened again by that sentence, “For you#super[+] know that afterward, when he wanted to inherit the blessing, he found no place of repentance, though he sought it carefully with tears.” So I could not return, but fled, though at times it cried, “Return, return,” as if it were calling out after me. But I feared to take hold of it, lest it should not come from God; for that other, as I said, was always sounding in my conscience, “For you#super[+] know that afterward, when he wanted to inherit the blessing, he was rejected,” and so on.

174\. Once, as I was walking to and fro in a good man’s shop, bemoaning myself in my sad and doleful state, afflicting myself with self-abhorrence for this wicked and ungodly thought, lamenting also this hard lot of mine, that I should commit so great a sin, greatly fearing I would not be pardoned, and praying also in my heart that, if this sin of mine differed from the sin against the Holy Spirit, the Lord would show it to me; and being now ready to sink with fear, suddenly there was, as if it had rushed in at the window, the noise of wind upon me, but very pleasant, and as if I had heard a voice speaking: “Did you ever refuse to be justified by the blood of Christ?” And with that my whole past life as a professing Christian was in a moment opened to me, and in it I was made to see that I had not done so on purpose; so my heart answered, groaning, “No.” Then that word of God fell upon me with power: “See that you#super[+] do not refuse Him who speaks” (Heb 12:25). This made a strange seizure upon my spirit; it brought light with it, and commanded a silence in my heart of all those tumultuous thoughts that before used, like masterless hell-hounds, to roar and bellow and make a hideous noise within me. It showed me also that Jesus Christ still had a word of grace and mercy for me, that He had not, as I had feared, quite forsaken and cast off my soul; yes, this was a kind of chiding for my proneness to desperation, a kind of threatening of me if I did not, notwithstanding my sins and their heinousness, venture my salvation upon the Son of God. But as to my judging about this strange dispensation, what it was I do not know, nor do I know where it came from. I have not yet, in twenty years’ time, been able to make a judgment of it; I #emph[thought then] what I #emph[would be] loath #emph[to speak] here. But truly, that sudden rushing wind was as if an angel had come upon me; but both it and the greeting I will leave until the day of judgment. Only this I say: it commanded a great calm in my soul; it persuaded me there might be hope; it showed me, as I thought, what the unpardonable sin was, and that my soul still had the blessed privilege of fleeing to Jesus Christ for mercy. But, I say, concerning this dispensation, I do not yet know what to say about it; and this was also, in truth, the reason that at first I did not speak of it in the book. I now also leave it to be thought on by men of sound judgment. I do not lay the weight of my salvation on it, but on the Lord Jesus, in the promise; yet, seeing I am here unfolding my secret things, I thought it might not be altogether unfitting to let this also show itself, though I cannot #emph[now] relate the matter as I experienced it there. This lasted, in the savor of it, for about three or four days, and then I began to mistrust and to despair again.

175\. So my life still hung in doubt before me, not knowing #emph[which way I should tip]\; only this I found my soul desire, even to cast itself at the foot of grace, by prayer and supplication. But oh! it was hard for me now to have the face to pray to this Christ for mercy, against whom I had most vilely sinned in this way; it was hard work, I say, to venture to look Him in the face against whom I had so vilely sinned; and indeed, I have found it as difficult to come to God by prayer, after backsliding from Him, as to do anything else. Oh, the shame that now came over me! especially when I thought, I am now going to pray to Him for mercy whom I had so lightly esteemed only a little while before! I was ashamed, yes, even confounded, because this villainy had been committed by me; but I saw that there was only one way for me: I must go to Him and humble myself before Him, and beg that He, of His wonderful mercy, would show pity to me, and have mercy on my wretched, sinful soul.

176\. When the tempter saw this, he strongly suggested to me that I ought not to pray to God, for prayer was not for anyone in my case, nor could it do me any good, because I had rejected the Mediator, by whom all prayers came with acceptance to God the Father, and without whom no prayer could come into His presence. So now to pray is only to add sin to sin; yes, now to pray, seeing God has cast you off, is the most direct way to anger and offend Him more than you ever did before.

177\. “For God,” he said, “has been weary of you these several years already, because you are not one of His; your bawlings in His ears have been no pleasant voice to Him; and therefore He let you sin this sin, that you might be quite cut off; and will you still pray?” This the devil urged, and he set before me how, in Numbers, Moses said to the children of Israel that because they would not go up to possess the land when God wanted them to, therefore He barred them out of it forever after, though they prayed with tears that they might go in (Num 14:36, 37, etc.).

178\. As it is said in another place (Exod 21:14), the man who sins presumptuously shall be taken from God’s altar, that he may die; just as Joab was by King Solomon, when he thought to find shelter there (1 Kgs 2:28, etc.). These places pinched me very sorely; yet, my case being desperate, I thought to myself, I can only die; and if it must be so, it shall be said of me once, “Such a one died at the foot of Christ in prayer.” This I did, but with great difficulty, God knows; and that because, together with this, that saying about Esau would continually be set at my heart, like a flaming sword, to keep the way of the tree of life, lest I should take of it and live. Oh! who knows how hard I found it to come to God in prayer?

179\. I also desired the prayers of the people of God for me, but I feared that God would give them no heart to do it; yes, I trembled in my soul to think that one or another of them would soon tell me that God had said to them the words He once said to the prophet concerning the children of Israel, “Do not pray for this people, for I have rejected them” (Jer 11:14). So: “Do not pray for him, for I have rejected him.” Yes, I thought that He had whispered this to some of them already, only they dared not tell me so, nor dared I ask them about it, for fear that, if it were so, it would drive me quite out of my mind. “Man knows the beginning of sin,” said Spira, “but who sets bounds to where it will end?”

180\. About this time I took an opportunity to open my mind to an old Christian, and told him my whole case; I told him also that I was afraid I had sinned the sin against the Holy Spirit; and he told me he thought so too. Here, therefore, I had only cold comfort; but, talking a little more with him, I found him, though a good man, a stranger to much combat with the devil. So I went to God again, as well as I could, for mercy still.

181\. Now also the tempter began to mock me in my misery, saying that, since I had parted with the Lord Jesus in this way, and provoked Him to displeasure, who would have stood between my soul and the flame of devouring fire, there was now only one way left, and that was to pray that God the Father would be the Mediator between His Son and me, that we might be reconciled again, and that I might have that blessed benefit in Him which His blessed saints enjoyed.

182\. Then that scripture seized on my soul, “He is of one mind, and who can turn Him?” Oh! I saw it was as easy to persuade Him to make a new world, a new covenant, or a new Bible, besides the one we have already, as to pray for such a thing. This would be to persuade Him that what He had already done was sheer folly, and to plead with Him to alter, yes, to annul, the whole way of salvation; and then that saying would tear my soul apart, “Salvation exists in no one else, for there is no other name under heaven given to men by which we must be saved” (Acts 4:12).

183\. Now the most free, and full, and gracious words of the gospel were the greatest torment to me; yes, nothing afflicted me so much as the thoughts of Jesus Christ, the remembrance of a Savior; because I had cast Him off, it brought to mind the villainy of my sin and my loss by it; nothing pricked my conscience like this. Every time I thought of the Lord Jesus, of His grace, love, goodness, kindness, gentleness, meekness, death, blood, promises and blessed exhortations, comforts and consolations, it went to my soul like a sword; for continually, alongside these my considerations of the Lord Jesus, these thoughts would make a place for themselves in my heart: “Yes, this is the Jesus, the loving Savior, the Son of God, whom you have parted with, whom you have slighted, despised, and abused. This is the only Savior, the only Redeemer, the only one who could so love sinners as to wash them from their sins in His own most precious blood; but you have no part or lot in this Jesus. You have put Him from you; you have said in your heart, ‘Let Him go if He will.’ Now, therefore, you are severed from Him; you have severed yourself from Him. See, then, His goodness, but yourself to be no partaker of it.” Oh, thought I, what have I lost! What have I parted with! What have I disinherited my poor soul of! Oh! it is sad to be destroyed by the grace and mercy of God; to have the Lamb, the Savior, turn lion and destroyer (Rev 6). I also trembled, as I have said, at the sight of the saints of God, especially at those who greatly loved Him, and who made it their business to walk continually with Him in this world; for they, in their words, their conduct, and all their expressions of tenderness and fear of sinning against their precious Savior, condemned my soul, laid guilt on it, and added continual affliction and shame to it. The dread of them was upon me, and I trembled at God’s Samuels (1 Sam 16:4).

184\. Now also the tempter began afresh to mock my soul another way, saying that Christ indeed pitied my case, and was sorry for my loss; but since I had sinned and transgressed as I had done, He could by no means help me, nor save me from what I feared; for my sin was not of the nature of theirs for whom He bled and died, nor was it counted with those that were laid to His charge when He hung on the tree. Therefore, unless He should come down from heaven and die anew for this sin, though He did indeed greatly pity me, yet I could have no benefit from Him. These things may seem ridiculous to others, even as ridiculous as they were in themselves, but to me they were most tormenting thoughts; every one of them increased my misery: that Jesus Christ should have so much love as to pity me when yet He could not help me. Nor did I think that the reason why He could not help me was that His merits were weak, or His grace and salvation already spent on others, but because His faithfulness to His threatening would not let Him extend His mercy to me. Besides, I thought, as I have already hinted, that my sin was not within the bounds of that pardon that was wrapped up in a promise; and if not, then I knew assuredly that it was easier for heaven and earth to pass away than for me to have eternal life. So the ground of all these fears of mine arose from a steadfast belief that I had in the stability of the holy Word of God, and also from my being misinformed about the nature of my sin.

185\. But oh! how this would add to my affliction, to imagine that I should be guilty of such a sin, for which He did not die. These thoughts would so confound me, and imprison me, and tie me up from faith, that I did not know what to do; but oh! thought I, if only He would come down again! Oh, if only the work of man’s redemption were yet to be done by Christ! How I would pray Him and entreat Him to count and reckon this sin among the rest for which He died! But this scripture would strike me down as dead, “Since Christ was raised from the dead, He cannot die again; death no longer has dominion over Him” (Rom 6:9).

186\. So, by the strange and unusual assaults of the tempter, my soul, like a broken vessel, was driven as with the winds, and tossed at times headlong into despair, at times onto the covenant of works, and at times to wishing that the new covenant and its conditions might, so far as I thought myself concerned, be turned another way and changed. #emph[But in all these I was only like those who dash against the rocks; more broken, scattered, and] torn. Oh, the unthought-of imaginations, frights, fears, and terrors that are brought about by a thorough application of guilt, yielding to desperation! This is the man who has his dwelling “in the tombs” with the dead; that is, always “crying out and cutting himself with stones” (Mark 5:2–5). But I say, all in vain; desperation will not comfort him, the old covenant will not save him; no, heaven and earth shall pass away before one jot or tittle of the Word and law of grace shall fail or be removed. This I saw, this I felt, and under this I groaned; yet this advantage I gained by it, namely, a further confirmation of the certainty of the way of salvation, and that the Scriptures were the Word of God! Oh! I cannot now express what I then saw and felt of the steadiness of Jesus Christ, the rock of man’s salvation; what was done could not be undone, added to, or altered. I saw, indeed, that sin might drive the soul beyond Christ, even the sin which is unpardonable; but woe to him who was so driven, for the Word would shut him out.

187\. So I was always sinking, whatever I thought or did. One day I walked to a neighboring town, and sat down on a bench in the street, and fell into a very deep pause about the most fearful state my sin had brought me to; and, after long musing, I lifted up my head, but it seemed to me as if the sun that shines in the heavens grudged to give light, and as if the very stones in the street, and the tiles on the houses, bent themselves against me; it seemed to me that they all combined together to banish me out of the world; I was abhorred by them, and unfit to dwell among them, or to be partaker of their benefits, because I had sinned against the Savior. Oh, how much happier now was every creature than I was! for they stood fast and kept their station, but I was gone and lost.

188\. Then, breaking out in the bitterness of my soul, I said to myself, with a grievous sigh, “How can God comfort such a wretch as I?” I had no sooner said it than this returned upon me, as an echo answers a voice, “This sin does not lead to death.” At this I was as if I had been raised out of a grave, and cried out again, “Lord, how could You find such a word as this?” For I was filled with wonder at the fitness, and also at the unexpectedness, of the sentence; the fitness of the word, the rightness of its timing, the power, and sweetness, and light, and glory that came with it, were also marvelous for me to find. I was now, for the time, out of doubt about that which I had been so much in doubt about before; my fears before #emph[were] that my sin was not pardonable, and so that I had no right to pray, to repent, and so on, or that if I did, it would be of no advantage or profit to me. But now, thought I, if #emph[this sin] does not lead to death, then it is pardonable; so from this I have encouragement to come to God, by Christ, for mercy, and to consider the promise of forgiveness as that which stands with open arms to receive me, as well as others. This, therefore, was a great easing to my mind; that is, that my sin was pardonable, that it was not the sin leading to death (1 John 5:16, 17). None but those who know by their own experience what my trouble was can tell what relief came to my soul from this consideration; it was a release from my former bonds, and a shelter from the former storm. I seemed now to stand on the same ground as other sinners, and to have as good a right to the word and to prayer as any of them.

189\. Now, I say, I had hopes that my sin was not unpardonable, but that there might be hope for me to obtain forgiveness. But oh, how Satan now laid about him to bring me down again! But he could by no means do it, neither that day nor most of the next, for this good sentence stood like a mill-post at my back; yet, toward the evening of the next day, I felt this word begin to leave me and to withdraw its support from me, and so I returned to my old fears again, but with a great deal of grudging and peevishness, for I feared the sorrow of despair; nor could my faith any longer retain this word.

190\. But the next day, at evening, being under many fears, I went to seek the Lord; and as I prayed, I cried, and my soul cried to Him in these words, with strong cries: “O Lord, I beg You, show me that You have loved me with an everlasting love” (Jer 31:3). I had no sooner said it than, with sweetness, this returned upon me, as an echo or sounding again, “I have loved you with an everlasting love.” Now I went to bed in quiet; also, when I woke the next morning, it was fresh upon my soul, and I believed it.

191\. But even so the tempter did not leave me; for it could not have been fewer than a hundred times that he labored that day to break my peace. Oh! the combats and conflicts that I then met with as I strove to hold on to this word; that of Esau would fly in my face like lightning. At times I would be up and down twenty times in an hour, yet God bore me up and kept my heart on this word, from which I also had, for several days together, very much sweetness and comforting hopes of pardon; for this is how it was made plain to me: “I loved you while you were committing this sin, I loved you before, I love you still, and I will love you forever.”

192\. Yet I saw my sin as most barbarous, and a filthy crime, and could not help concluding, and that with great shame and astonishment, that I had horribly abused the holy Son of God; so I felt my soul greatly love and pity Him, and my heart yearn toward Him; for I saw He was still my Friend, and rewarded me good for evil; yes, the love and affection that then burned within me toward my Lord and Savior Jesus Christ worked, at this time, such a strong and hot desire for revenge on myself for the abuse I had done to Him, that, to speak as I then thought, had I had a thousand gallons of blood in my veins, I could freely have spilled it all then at the command and feet of this my Lord and Savior.

193\. And as I was thus musing and deep in thought, considering how to love the Lord and to express my love to Him, that saying came in upon me, “If You, O #smallcaps[Lord], kept track of iniquities, then who, O Lord, could stand? But with You there is forgiveness, so that You may be feared” (Ps 130:3, 4). These were good words to me, especially the latter part; that is, that there is forgiveness with the Lord, that He might be feared; that is, as I then understood it, that He might be loved and held in reverence; for it was made plain to me in this way: that the great God set so high a value on the love of His poor creatures that, rather than go without their love, He would pardon their transgressions.

194\. And now that word was fulfilled in me, and I was also refreshed by it, “Then they shall be ashamed and confounded, and never again open their mouth because of their shame, when I am pacified toward them for all they have done, declares the Lord #smallcaps[God]” (Ezek 16:63). So my soul at this time, and, as I then thought, forever, was set free from being afflicted with my former guilt and dismay.

195\. But before many weeks were over I began to lose heart again, fearing that, in spite of all I had enjoyed, I might yet be deceived and destroyed at the last; for this consideration came strongly into my mind: that whatever comfort and peace I thought I might have from the word of the promise of life, yet unless my refreshment could be found to have the concurrence and agreement of the Scriptures, let me think what I would of it, and hold it ever so fast, I should find no such thing at the end; “for Scripture cannot be broken” (John 10:35).

196\. Now my heart began again to ache and fear that I might meet with disappointment at the last; so I began, with all seriousness, to examine my former comfort, and to consider whether one who had sinned as I had done might with confidence trust in the faithfulness of God, laid down in those words by which I had been comforted and on which I had leaned. But now those sayings were brought to my mind, “For it is impossible for those who were once enlightened, and have tasted the heavenly gift, and were made partakers of the Holy Spirit, and have tasted the good word of God and the powers of the age to come, if they fall away, to renew them again to repentance” (Heb 6:4–6). “For if we sin willfully after we have received the knowledge of the truth, there remains no more sacrifice for sin, but a certain fearful expectation of judgment and of raging fire that will consume the adversaries” (Heb 10:26, 27). Even “like Esau, who for one morsel of food sold his birthright. For you#super[+] know that afterward, when he wanted to inherit the blessing, he was rejected; for he found no place of repentance, though he sought it carefully with tears” (Heb 12:16, 17).

197\. Now the word of the gospel was forced from my soul, so that no promise or encouragement was to be found in the Bible for me; and now that saying would work on my spirit to afflict me, “Do not rejoice, O Israel, with exultation like the nations” (Hos 9:1). For I saw indeed there was cause of rejoicing for those who held to Jesus; but as for me, I had cut myself off by my transgressions, and left myself neither foothold nor handhold among all the stays and props in the precious word of life.

198\. And truly I now felt myself sink into a gulf, like a house whose foundation is destroyed; I likened myself, in this condition, to the case of some child that had fallen into a mill-pit, who, though it could make some shift to scrabble and sprawl in the water, yet because it could find no hold for hand or foot, must at last die in that condition. As soon as this fresh assault had fastened on my soul, that scripture came into my heart, “This is for many days” (Dan 10:14). And indeed I found it was so; for I could not be delivered, nor brought to peace again, until nearly two and a half years were completely finished. So these words, though in themselves they tended to discouragement, yet to me, who feared this condition would be eternal, were at times a help and refreshment.

199\. For, thought I, #emph[many days] are not forever, #emph[many days] will have an end; therefore, seeing I was to be afflicted, not a few, but many #emph[days], yet I was glad it was only for many #emph[days]. In this way, I say, I could at times bring myself back and give myself some help, for as soon as ever the words first came into my mind, I knew my trouble would be long; yet this would be only at times, for I could not always think on this, nor always be helped by it, even when I did.

200\. Now while these scriptures lay before me, and laid sin anew at my door, that saying in the eighteenth of Luke, with others, encouraged me to pray. Then the tempter again laid at me very sorely, suggesting that neither the mercy of God nor the blood of Christ concerned me at all, nor could they help me for my sin; therefore it was only in vain to pray. Yet, thought I, “I #emph[will] pray.” “But,” said the tempter, “your sin is unpardonable.” “Well,” said I, “I #emph[will] pray.” “It is no use,” said he. “Yet,” said I, “I #emph[will] pray.” So I went to prayer to God; and while I was at prayer, I spoke words to this effect: “Lord, Satan tells me that neither Your mercy nor Christ’s blood is sufficient to save my soul. Lord, shall I honor You most by believing You will and can? or him, by believing You neither will nor can? Lord, I would gladly honor You by believing You will and can.”

201\. And as I was thus before the Lord, that scripture fastened on my heart, “O man, your faith is great” (Matt 15:28), as if someone had clapped me on the back as I was on my knees before God. #emph[Yet] I was not able to believe this, that this was a prayer of faith, till almost six months after; for I could not think that I had faith, or that there should be a word for me to act faith on; therefore I would continually be as one sticking in the jaws of desperation, and went mourning up and down in a sad condition, crying, “Is His mercy clean gone? Is His mercy clean gone forever?” And I thought at times, even when I was groaning out these words, that they seemed to make a question of whether it was or not; yet I greatly feared it was.

202\. There was nothing I now longed for more than to be put out of doubt about this question; and, as I was earnestly desiring to know if there was indeed hope for me, these words came rolling into my mind, “Will the Lord cast off forever and never show His favor again? Is His mercy clean gone forever? Has His promise failed for all time? Has God forgotten to be gracious? Has He in anger shut up His tender mercies?” (Ps 77:7–9). And all the while they ran in my mind, I thought I continually had this as the answer: “It is a question whether He has or not; it may be He has not.” Yes, the question seemed to me to carry in it a sure affirmation that indeed He had not, nor would so cast off, but would show favor; that His promise does not fail, and that He had not forgotten to be gracious, nor would in anger shut up His tender mercies. There was also something on my heart at the same time which I cannot now call to mind, which, with this text, sweetened my heart, and made me conclude that His mercy might not be quite gone, nor clean gone forever.

203\. At another time, I remember, I was again much under this question, “Is the blood of Christ sufficient to save my soul?” In this doubt I continued from morning till about seven or eight at night; and at last, when I was, as it were, quite worn out with fear that it would not lay hold on me, these words suddenly sounded within my heart, “He is #emph[able].” But it seemed to me this word #emph[able] was spoken loudly to me; it showed a #emph[great] word, it seemed to be written in great letters, and gave such a jolt to my fear and doubt (I mean for the time it stayed with me, which was about a day) as I never had from that in all my life, either before or after (Heb 7:25).

204\. But one morning, when I was again at prayer, and trembling under the fear that no word of God could help me, that piece of a sentence darted in upon me, “#emph[My] grace is sufficient.” At this I thought I felt some stay, as if there might be hope. But oh, how good a thing it is for God to send His word! For about a fortnight before, I was looking at this very place, and then I thought it could not come near my soul with comfort; so I threw down my book in a fit of temper. Then I thought it was not large enough for me; no, not large enough; but now it was as if it had arms of grace so wide that it could enclose not only me, but many more such as I besides.

205\. By these words I was sustained, yet not without exceeding conflicts, for the space of seven or eight weeks; for my peace would be in it and out, at times twenty times a day; comfort now, and trouble a moment later; peace now, and before I could go a furlong as full of fear and guilt as ever heart could hold; and this was not only now and then, but my whole seven weeks’ experience; for this about the sufficiency of grace, and that about Esau’s parting with his birthright, would be like a pair of scales within my mind; sometimes one end would be uppermost, and sometimes again the other; and according to which it was would be my peace or trouble.

206\. So I continually prayed to God that He would come in with this scripture more fully on my heart; that is, that He would help me to apply the whole sentence, for as yet I could not. What He gave, I gathered; but further I could not go, for as yet it only helped me to hope there might be mercy for me, “My grace is sufficient”; and though it went no further, it answered my former question, namely, that there was hope; yet, because “for you” was left out, I was not content, but prayed to God for that also. So one day, as I was in a meeting of God’s people, full of sadness and terror, for my fears were strong upon me again, and as I was now thinking my soul was never the better, but my case most sad and fearful, these words suddenly broke in upon me with great power, “My grace is sufficient for you, my grace is sufficient for you, my grace is sufficient for you,” three times together; and oh! I thought that every word was a mighty word to me, as “my,” and “grace,” and “sufficient,” and “for you”; they were then, and at times still are, far bigger than others are.

207\. At that time my understanding was so enlightened that it was as though I had seen the Lord Jesus look down from heaven through the tiles upon me, and direct these words to me. This sent me home mourning; it broke my heart, and filled me full of joy, and laid me as low as the dust; only it did not stay long with me, I mean in this glory and refreshing comfort, yet it continued with me for several weeks, and encouraged me to hope. But as soon as that powerful working of it was taken off my heart, that other saying about Esau returned upon me as before; so my soul hung as in a pair of scales again, sometimes up and sometimes down, now in peace, and soon again in terror.

208\. So I went on for many weeks, sometimes comforted, and sometimes tormented; and especially at some times my torment would be very sore, for all those scriptures named before in Hebrews would be set before me, as the only sentences that would keep me out of heaven. Then again I would begin to repent that that thought ever went through me; I would also think this way with myself, “Why, how many scriptures are there against me? There are only three or four; and cannot God miss them, and save me in spite of them all?” At times, again, I would think, “Oh! if it were not for these three or four words, how I might be comforted now!” And I could hardly keep myself, at some times, from wishing them out of the book.

209\. Then it seemed to me as if both Peter, and Paul, and John, and all the writers, looked on me with scorn and held me in derision; and as if they said to me, “All our words are truth, one of as much force as another. It is not we who have cut you off, but you have cast yourself away; there is none of our sentences that you must take hold of but these, and such as these: ‘It is impossible’; ‘there remains no more sacrifice for sin’ (Heb 6). And ‘it would have been better for them not to have known’ the will of God ‘than to have known it and then to turn away from the holy commandment passed on to them’ (2 Pet 2:21). ‘For Scripture cannot be broken.’”

210\. These, like the elders of the city of refuge, I saw were to be the judges both of my case and of me, while I stood, with the avenger of blood at my heels, trembling at their gate for deliverance; also, with a thousand fears and mistrusts, I feared that they would shut me out forever (Josh 20:3, 4).

211\. So I was confounded, not knowing what to do, nor how to be satisfied in this question, “Could the Scriptures agree in the salvation of my soul?” I quaked at the apostles; I knew their words were true, and that they must stand forever.

212\. And I remember one day, as I was in various frames of spirit, and considering that these frames were always according to the nature of the separate scriptures that came in upon my mind: if this one of grace, then I was quiet; but if that one of Esau, then tormented. “Lord,” thought I, “if both these scriptures would meet in my heart at once, I wonder which of them would get the better of me.” So it seemed to me I had a longing mind that they might both come upon me together; yes, I desired of God that they might.

213\. Well, about two or three days after, so they did indeed; they bolted upon me both at once, and worked and struggled strangely in me for a while; at last, that about Esau’s birthright began to grow weak, and withdraw, and vanish, and this about the sufficiency of grace prevailed, with peace and joy. And as I was musing about this, that scripture came home to me, “Mercy triumphs over judgment” (Jas 2:13).

214\. This was a wonderment to me; yet truly I am inclined to think it was of God; for the word of the law and wrath must give place to the word of life and grace; because, though the word of condemnation is glorious, yet the word of life and salvation far exceeds it in glory (2 Cor 3:8–12; Mark 9:5–7). Also, Moses and Elijah must both vanish, and leave Christ and His saints alone.

215\. This scripture also now most sweetly visited my soul, “And him who comes to Me I will by no means cast out” (John 6:37). Oh, the comfort I had from this word, “by no means”! as if he should say, in no way, not for anything, whatever he has done. But Satan would labor hard to pull this promise from me, telling me that Christ did not mean me, and such as I, but sinners of a lower rank, who had not done as I had done. But I would answer him again, “Satan, there is no such exception in these words; but ‘him who comes’, #emph[him], #emph[any him]: ‘him who comes to Me I will by no means cast out.’” And I still remember this well, that of all the tricks Satan used to take this scripture from me, he never once put this question, “But do you come aright?” And I have thought the reason was that he thought I knew full well what coming aright was; for I saw that to come aright was to come as I was, a vile and ungodly sinner, and to cast myself at the feet of mercy, condemning myself for sin. If ever Satan and I strove for any word of God in all my life, it was for this good word of Christ; he at one end and I at the other. Oh, what work we made! It was for this in John, I say, that we tugged and strove so; he pulled and I pulled; but, God be praised, I got the better of him, I got some sweetness from it.

216\. But notwithstanding all these helps and blessed words of grace, yet that of Esau’s selling his birthright would still at times distress my conscience; for though I had been most sweetly comforted, and that only just before, yet when that came into my mind, it would make me fear again; I could not be quite rid of it; it would be with me every day. So now I went to work another way, namely, to consider the nature of this blasphemous thought; I mean, if I should take the words at their largest, and give them their own natural force and scope, every word in them. When I had considered them this way, I found that if they were fairly taken, they would amount to this: that I had freely left the Lord Jesus Christ to His choice, whether He would be my Savior or not; for the wicked words were these, “Let Him go if He #emph[will].” Then that scripture gave me hope, “Never will I leave you, never will I forsake you” (Heb 13:5). “O Lord,” said I, “but I have #emph[left You].” Then it answered again, “But I #emph[will not] leave you.” For this I thank God also.

217\. #emph[Yet] I was grievously afraid He would, and found it exceedingly hard to trust Him, seeing I had so offended Him. I would have been exceedingly glad if this thought had never befallen me, for then I thought I could, with much more ease and freedom, have leaned on His grace. I see it was with me as it was with Joseph’s brothers; the guilt of their own wickedness often filled them with fears that their brother would at last despise them (Gen 50:15–17).

218\. Yet above all the scriptures that I had yet met with, that in the twentieth of Joshua was the greatest comfort to me, which speaks of the slayer who was to flee for refuge. “And if the avenger of blood pursues the slayer, then,” says Moses, “those who are the elders of the city of refuge shall not deliver him into his hand, because he struck his neighbor unwittingly, and did not hate him beforehand.” Oh, blessed be God for this word; I was convinced that I was the slayer; and that the avenger of blood pursued me, I felt with great terror; only now it remained for me to inquire whether I had a right to enter the city of refuge. So I found that he must not enter #emph[who] lay in wait to #emph[shed blood]: it was not the willful #emph[murderer], but he who did it #emph[unwittingly], he who did it unawares; not out of spite, or grudge, or malice; he who shed it unwittingly, even he who did not #emph[hate his neighbor before]. Therefore:

219\. I thought surely I was the man who must enter, because I had struck my neighbor #emph[unwittingly, and did not hate him] beforehand. I did not hate Him beforehand; no, I prayed to Him, and was tender of sinning against Him; yes, and against this wicked temptation I had striven for a year before; yes, and also when it did pass through my heart, it did so in spite of all I could do. So I thought I had a right to enter this city, and the elders, who are the apostles, were not to deliver me up. This, therefore, was a great comfort to me, and gave me much ground of hope.

220\. Yet being very exacting, for my pain had made me so that I did not know what ground was firm enough to bear me, I had one question that my soul greatly desired to be resolved about, and that was, “Is it possible for any soul that has indeed sinned the unpardonable sin to receive after that even the least true spiritual comfort from God through Christ?” After I had considered it much, I found the answer was, No, they could not; and that for these reasons:

221\. First, because those who have sinned that sin are barred from a share in the blood of Christ, and being shut out of that, they must be without the least ground of hope, and so of spiritual comfort; for to such “there remains no more sacrifice for sin” (Heb 10:26). Secondly, because they are denied a share in the promise of life; they shall never be forgiven, “neither in this world nor in the world to come” (Matt 12:32). Thirdly, the Son of God also excludes them from a share in His blessed intercession, being forever ashamed to acknowledge them both before His holy Father and the blessed angels in heaven (Mark 8:38).

222\. When I had considered this matter with much deliberation, and could not but conclude that the Lord had comforted me, and that too after this wicked sin of mine, then I thought I dared venture to come near those most fearful and terrible scriptures with which all this while I had been so greatly frightened. Before this I had scarcely dared cast my eye on them; indeed, a hundred times I could hardly keep from wishing them out of the Bible, for I thought they would destroy me. But now, I say, I began to take some measure of encouragement to come close to them, to read them and consider them, and to weigh their scope and tendency.

223\. When I began to do this, I found their face changed; for they did not look so grim as I had thought they did before. And first, I came to the sixth of Hebrews, still trembling for fear it would strike me. When I had considered it, I found that the falling there intended was a falling #emph[quite] away; that is, as I understood it, a falling from, and an absolute denying of, the gospel of the forgiveness of sins by Christ; for the apostle begins his argument from these (vv. 1–3). Secondly, I found that this falling away must be done openly, even in the view of the world, even so as “to put Christ to an open shame.” Thirdly, I found that those he there intended were forever shut up by God, in blindness, hardness, and impenitence: it is “impossible they should be renewed again to repentance.” By all these particulars I found, to God’s everlasting praise, that my sin was not the sin intended in this place.

First, I confessed I was fallen, but not fallen away, that is, from the profession of faith in Jesus to eternal life.

Secondly, I confessed that I had put Jesus Christ to #emph[shame] by my sin, but not to open #emph[shame]\; I did not deny Him before men, nor condemn Him as a fruitless one before the world.

Thirdly, nor did I find that God had shut me up, or refused to let me come, though I found it hard work indeed to come to Him by sorrow and repentance. Blessed be God for unsearchable grace.

224\. Then I considered the passage in the tenth of Hebrews, and found that the #emph[willful sin] mentioned there is not every willful sin, but that sin which throws off Christ, and then His commandments too. Secondly, it must also be done openly, before two or three witnesses, to answer to what the law required (v. 28). Thirdly, this sin cannot be committed except with great insult done to the Spirit of grace, despising both the warnings against that sin and the persuasions to the contrary. But the Lord knows that, though this sin of mine was devilish, it did not amount to these.

225\. And as for the passage in the twelfth of Hebrews, about Esau’s selling his birthright, though this was what had killed me, and stood like a spear against me, yet now I considered: First, that his was not a hasty thought against the continual labor of his mind, but a thought consented to and put into practice as well, and that too after some deliberation (Gen 25). Secondly, it was a public and open action, before his brother at least, if not before many more; this made his sin of a far more heinous nature than it would otherwise have been. Thirdly, he continued to slight his birthright: “He ate and drank and then got up and went away. Thus Esau despised his birthright” (v. 34). Indeed, twenty years after, he was found to despise it still: “And Esau said, ‘I already have plenty, my brother. Keep what belongs to you’” (Gen 33:9).

226\. Now as to this, that Esau sought a place of repentance, this is what I thought. First, this was not for the birthright, #emph[but for the blessing]\; this is clear from the apostle, and is distinguished by Esau himself: “He took my birthright” (that is, formerly), “and now he has taken my blessing” (Gen 27:36). Secondly, having considered this, I came again to the apostle, to see what might be the mind of God, in a New Testament style and sense, concerning Esau’s sin. So far as I could understand, this was the mind of God: that the birthright signified #emph[regeneration], and the blessing #emph[the eternal inheritance]. For so the apostle seems to hint: “Lest there be any profane person, like Esau, who for one morsel of food sold his birthright”; as if he should say, Lest there be any person among you who casts off all those blessed beginnings of God that are now upon him, in order to a new birth, lest they become like Esau, and be rejected #emph[afterward], when they want to inherit the blessing.

227\. For there are many who, in the day of grace and mercy, despise those things which are indeed the birthright to heaven, who yet, when the deciding day appears, will cry as loud as Esau, “Lord, Lord, open to us.” But then, as Isaac would not repent, no more will God the Father, but will say, “I have blessed these, and indeed, they will be blessed; but as for you#super[+], depart, you are workers of iniquity” (Gen 27:33; Luke 13:25–27).

228\. When I had considered these scriptures in this way, and found that to understand them so was not against other scriptures but in agreement with them, this added still further to my encouragement and comfort. It also gave a great blow to that objection, namely, that the Scriptures could not agree in the salvation of my soul. And now only the tail end of the tempest remained, for the thunder had gone beyond me; only some drops still remained, which now and then would fall on me. But because my former frights and anguish had been very sore and deep, it often happened to me still, as it happens to those who have been scared by fire, that I thought every voice was “Fire! fire!” Every little touch would hurt my tender conscience.

229\. But one day, as I was passing through the field, and that too with some blows upon my conscience, fearing that all was still not right, suddenly this sentence fell upon my soul: “Your righteousness is in heaven.” And I thought, along with it, that I saw with the eyes of my soul Jesus Christ at God’s right hand. There, I say, was my righteousness; so that wherever I was, or whatever I was doing, God could not say of me, “He lacks My righteousness,” for that was right before Him. I also saw, moreover, that it was not my good frame of heart that made my righteousness better, nor yet my bad frame that made my righteousness worse; for my righteousness was Jesus Christ Himself, “the same yesterday and today and forever” (Heb 13:8).

230\. Now my chains did indeed fall off my legs; I was loosed from my afflictions and irons; my temptations also fled away. So from that time those dreadful scriptures of God left off troubling me. Now I also went home rejoicing for the grace and love of God. So when I came home, I looked to see if I could find that sentence, “Your righteousness is in heaven,” but could not find such a saying, and so my heart began to sink again. Only this was brought to my remembrance: “He has become for us wisdom from God: our righteousness, sanctification, and redemption.” By this word I saw that the other sentence was true (1 Cor 1:30).

231\. For by this scripture I saw that the man Christ Jesus, as He is distinct from us in His bodily presence, is our righteousness and sanctification before God. Here, therefore, I lived for some time, very sweetly at peace with God through Christ. Oh, I thought, Christ! Christ! There was nothing but Christ before my eyes. I was not now only for looking at this and that benefit of Christ separately, such as His blood, burial, or resurrection, but considered Him as a whole Christ! As He in whom all these, and all His other virtues, relations, offices, and works, met together, and that as He sat at the right hand of God in heaven.

232\. It was glorious to me to see His exaltation, and the worth and prevailing power of all His benefits, and that because now I could look from myself to Him, and would reckon that all those graces of God that were now green in me were still only like those cracked groats and fourpence-halfpennies that rich men carry in their purses, while their gold is in their trunks at home! Oh, I saw my gold was in my trunk at home! In Christ, my Lord and Savior! Now Christ was all: all my wisdom, all my righteousness, all my sanctification, and all my redemption.

233\. Further, the Lord also led me into the mystery of union with the Son of God: that I was joined to Him, that I was flesh of His flesh and bone of His bone; and now that was a sweet word to me in Ephesians 5:30. By this also my faith in Him as my righteousness was the more confirmed in me; for if He and I were one, then His righteousness was mine, His merits mine, His victory also mine. Now I could see myself in heaven and on earth at once: in heaven by my Christ, by my head, by my righteousness and life, though on earth by my body or person.

234\. Now I saw that Christ Jesus was looked on by God, and should also be looked on by us, as that common or public person in whom the whole body of His elect are always to be considered and reckoned: that we fulfilled the law by Him, died by Him, rose from the dead by Him, and got the victory over sin, death, the devil, and hell by Him. When He died, we died; and so of His resurrection. “Your dead will live; together with my dead body they will rise,” he says (Isa 26:19). And again, “After two days He will revive us; and on the third day we will live in His presence” (Hos 6:2). This is now fulfilled by the sitting down of the Son of Man at the right hand of the Majesty in the heavens, according to that word to the Ephesians, “He raised us up with Christ and seated us with Him in the heavenly realms in Christ Jesus” (Eph 2:6).

235\. Ah, these blessed considerations and scriptures, with many others of a like nature, were in those days made to spangle in my eyes, so that I have cause to say, “Hallelujah! Praise God in His sanctuary. Praise Him in His mighty heavens. Praise Him for His mighty acts; praise Him for His excellent greatness” (Ps 150:1, 2).

236\. I have thus, in a few words, given you a taste of the sorrow and affliction my soul went through under the guilt and terror that this wicked thought of mine laid on me; and I have also given you a touch of my deliverance from it, and of the sweet and blessed comfort I met with afterward, a comfort which dwelt with my heart for about a year, to my unspeakable wonder. Now, God willing, before I go any further, I will give you in a word or two what I believe was the cause of this temptation, and also, after that, what advantage it became to my soul in the end.

237\. As for the causes, I believed they were chiefly two, and of these two I was deeply convinced all the time this trouble lay on me. The first was that, when I was delivered from the temptation that went before, I did not #emph[continue to pray] to God to keep #emph[me from temptations that were to come]. For though, as I can say in truth, my soul was much in prayer before this trial seized me, yet then I prayed only, or at most chiefly, for the removal of present troubles and for fresh revelations of His love in Christ. This, I saw afterward, was not enough; I should also have prayed that the great God would keep me from the evil that was to come.

238\. Of this I was made deeply aware by the prayer of holy David, who, when he was under present mercy, still prayed that God would hold him back from sin and temptation to come. “Then,” he says, “I will be blameless and innocent of the great transgression” (Ps 19:13). By this very word I was galled and condemned, all through this long temptation.

239\. Another word that much condemned me for my folly in neglecting this duty was this: “Let us then approach the throne of grace with confidence, so that we may receive mercy and find grace to help us in our time of need” (Heb 4:16). This I had not done, and therefore I was allowed to sin and fall in this way, according to what is written, “Pray that you#super[+] will not enter into temptation.” And truly this very thing lies on me to this day with such weight and awe that, when I come before the Lord, I dare not get up off my knees until I entreat Him for help and mercy against the temptations that are to come. And I beg you, reader, to learn to beware of my negligence, by the afflictions I underwent with sorrow for this very thing, for days, and months, and years.

240\. Another cause of this temptation was that I had tempted God, and this is how I did it. Once, when my wife was with child, before her full time had come, her pangs, like those of a woman in labor, were fierce and strong upon her, as if she would immediately have gone into labor and been delivered of an untimely birth. Now it was at this very time that I had been so strongly tempted to question the being of God. So, as my wife lay crying beside me, I said, but with all the secrecy imaginable, only thinking it in my heart, “Lord, if You will now remove this sad affliction from my wife, and cause her to be troubled no more with it this night” (and now her pangs were just upon her), “then I shall know that You can discern the most secret thoughts of the heart.”

241\. I had no sooner said it in my heart than her pangs were taken from her, and she was cast into a deep sleep, and so continued till morning. At this I greatly marveled, not knowing what to think; but after I had been awake a good while, and heard her cry no more, I fell asleep too. So when I woke in the morning, it came upon me again, what I had said in my heart the night before, and how the Lord had shown me that He knew my secret thoughts. This was a great astonishment to me for several weeks after.

242\. Well, about a year and a half afterward, that wicked, sinful thought of which I have spoken before went through my wicked heart, this very thought: “Let Christ go if He will.” So when I had fallen under guilt for this, the remembrance of my other thought, and of its effect, would also come upon me with this retort, which also carried rebuke along with it: “Now you may see that God does know the most secret thoughts of the heart.”

243\. And with this, what passed between the Lord and His servant Gideon fell upon my spirit: how, because Gideon tempted God with his fleece, both wet and dry, when he should have believed and ventured on His word, the Lord afterward tried him by sending him against an innumerable company of enemies, and that too, as far as outward appearance went, without any strength or help (Judg 6–7). So He dealt with me, and justly, for I should have believed His word, and not have put an “if” upon the all-seeingness of God.

244\. And now to show you something of the advantages that I have also gained by this temptation. And first, by this I was made continually to possess in my soul a very wonderful sense both of the being and glory of God and of His beloved Son. In the temptation that went before, my soul was perplexed with unbelief, blasphemy, hardness of heart, and questions about the being of God and Christ, the truth of the Word, and the certainty of the world to come; I say, then I was greatly assaulted and tormented with atheism. But now the case was otherwise; now God and Christ were continually before my face, though not in a way of comfort, but in a way of exceeding dread and terror. The glory of the holiness of God at this time broke me to pieces, and the tender heart and compassion of Christ broke me as on the wheel; for I could not consider Him except as a lost and rejected Christ, and the remembrance of this was like the continual breaking of my bones.

245\. The Scriptures also were wonderful things to me. I saw that the truth and certainty of them were the keys of the kingdom of heaven: #emph[those] whom the Scriptures favor #emph[they] must inherit bliss, but #emph[those] whom they oppose and condemn #emph[must] perish forevermore. Oh! this word, “the Scripture cannot be broken,” would tear the caul of my heart; and so would that other, “If you#super[+] forgive anyone his sins, they are forgiven; if you withhold forgiveness from anyone, it is withheld.” Now I saw the apostles to be the elders of the city of refuge (Josh 20:4): those whom they were to receive in were received to life, but those whom they shut out were to be slain by the avenger of blood.

246\. Oh! one sentence of the Scripture afflicted and terrified my mind more (I mean those sentences that stood against me, as at times I thought every one of them did), more, I say, than an army of forty thousand men that might have come against me. Woe to him against whom the Scriptures bend themselves!

247\. By this temptation I was made to see more into the nature of the promises than ever I had before. For now I lay trembling under the mighty hand of God, continually torn and rent by the thundering of His justice; and this made me, with a careful heart and watchful eye, with great fearfulness, turn over every leaf, and with much diligence, mixed with trembling, consider every sentence, together with its natural force and scope.

248\. By this temptation, also, I was greatly kept from my former foolish practice of putting aside the word of promise when it came into my mind. For now, though I could not suck that comfort and sweetness from the promise as I had done at other times, yet, like a sinking man, I would catch at all I saw. Formerly I thought I might not meddle with the promise unless I felt its comfort; but now it was no time to do so, for the avenger of blood pursued me too hard.

249\. Now, therefore, I was glad to catch at #emph[that] word which I still feared I had no ground or right to claim, and even to leap into the bosom of that promise which I still feared shut its heart against me. Now also I would labor to take the Word as God has laid it down, without restraining the natural force of one syllable of it. Oh, what did I now see in that blessed sixth of John, “And him who comes to Me I will by no means cast out” (v. 37). Now I began to consider with myself that God had a bigger mouth to speak with than I had a heart to conceive with. I thought also that He did not speak His words in haste, or in an unadvised heat, but with infinite wisdom and judgment, and in very truth and faithfulness (2 Sam 7:28).

250\. In these days, often in my greatest agonies, I would even flounder toward the promise, as horses that are still stuck in the mire flounder toward sound ground, concluding (though as one almost robbed of his wits through fear), On this I will rest and stay, and leave the fulfilling of it to the God of heaven who made it. Oh! many a pull my heart has had with Satan for that blessed sixth of John. I did not now, as at other times, look chiefly for comfort (though, oh, how welcome it would have been to me!), but now a word, a word to lean a weary soul on, that I might not sink forever! That was what I hunted for.

251\. Indeed, often when I have been making for the promise, I have seen it as if the Lord would refuse my soul forever. I was often as if I had run upon the pikes, and as if the Lord had thrust at me, as with a flaming sword, to keep me from Him. Then I would think of Esther, who went to petition the king contrary to the law (Esth 4:16). I thought also of Benhadad’s servants, who went with ropes upon their heads to their enemies for mercy (1 Kgs 20:31). The woman of Canaan also, who would not be daunted though Christ called her a dog (Matt 15:21–28), and the man who went to borrow bread at midnight (Luke 11:5–8), were great encouragements to me.

252\. I never saw those heights and depths in grace, and love, and mercy, as I saw after this temptation. Great sins draw out great grace; and where guilt is most terrible and fierce, there the mercy of God in Christ, when shown to the soul, appears most high and mighty. When Job had passed through his captivity, the Lord “doubled his former possessions” (Job 42:10). Blessed be God for Jesus Christ our Lord. Many other things I might observe here, but I would be brief, and therefore shall omit them at this time; and I pray God that my harms may make others fear to offend, lest they also be made to bear the iron yoke as I did.

I had two or three times, at or about my deliverance from this temptation, such strange apprehensions of the grace of God that I could hardly bear up under it; it was so beyond measure amazing, when I thought it could reach me, that I do think, if that sense of it had stayed long upon me, it would have made me unfit for business.

253\. Now I shall go on to give you an account of other dealings of the Lord with me, of His dealings with me at various other seasons, and of the temptations I met with then. I shall begin with what I met with when I first joined in fellowship with the people of God in Bedford. After I had proposed to the church that my desire was to walk with them in the order and ordinances of Christ, and was also admitted by them, while I thought of that blessed ordinance of Christ which was His last supper with His disciples before His death, that scripture, “Do this in remembrance of Me” (Luke 22:19), was made a very precious word to me. For by it the Lord came down upon my conscience with the revelation of His death for my sins, and, as I then felt, it was as if He plunged me in its virtue. But behold, I had not been a partaker at that ordinance long before such fierce and sad temptations attended me at all times in it, both to blaspheme the ordinance and to wish some deadly thing on those who were then eating of it, that, lest I should at any time be guilty of consenting to these wicked and fearful thoughts, I was forced to bend myself all the while to pray to God to keep me from such blasphemies, and also to cry to God to bless the bread and cup to them as it went from mouth to mouth. The reason for this temptation, I have thought since, was that I did not at first approach to partake of it with the reverence that became me.

254\. I continued like this for three-quarters of a year, and could never have rest or ease; but at last the Lord came in upon my soul with the same scripture by which my soul had been visited before. And after that I have usually been very well and comforted in partaking of that blessed ordinance, and have, I trust, discerned in it the #emph[Lord’s body] as broken for my sins, and that His precious blood has been shed for my transgressions.

255\. Once I was somewhat inclined to a consumption, and with it, about the spring, I was suddenly and violently seized with much weakness in my outward man, so much that I thought I could not live. Now I began afresh to give myself up to a serious examination of my state and condition for the future, and of my evidences for that blessed world to come. For it has been my usual course, I bless the name of God, always, but especially in the day of affliction, to try to keep my interest in the life to come clear before my eyes.

256\. But I had no sooner begun to recall to mind my former experience of the goodness of God to my soul than there came flocking into my mind an innumerable company of my sins and transgressions. Among these, the ones that afflicted me most at this time were my deadness, dullness, and coldness in holy duties; my wanderings of heart; my weariness in all good things; my lack of love to God, His ways, and His people; with this at the end of all: “Are these the fruits of Christianity? Are these the tokens of a blessed man?”

257\. At the apprehension of these things my sickness was doubled upon me, for now I was sick in my inward man; my soul was clogged with guilt. Now also all my former experience of God’s goodness to me was quite taken out of my mind, and hidden as if it had never been or been seen. Now my soul was greatly pinched between these two considerations: “Live I must not; die I dare not.” Now I sank and fell in my spirit, and was giving up all for lost. But as I was walking up and down in the house, as a man in a most woeful state, that word of God took hold of my heart, “You#super[+] are justified freely by His grace through the redemption that is in Christ Jesus” (Rom 3:24). But oh, what a turn it made upon me!

258\. Now I was like one awakened out of some troublesome sleep and dream, and, listening to this heavenly sentence, it was as if I heard it explained to me like this: “Sinner, you think that because of your sins and infirmities I cannot save your soul; but behold, My Son is by Me, and I look upon Him, and not on you, and will deal with you according as I am pleased with Him.” At this I was greatly lightened in my mind, and made to understand that God could justify a sinner at any time; it was only His looking upon Christ, and imputing His benefits to us, and the work was done at once.

259\. And as I was musing like this, that scripture also came with great power upon my spirit, “He saved us, not by the righteous deeds we had done, but according to His mercy,” etc. (Titus 3:5; 2 Tim 1:9). Now I was raised on high; I saw myself within the arms of grace and mercy. And though before I had been afraid to think of a dying hour, yet now I cried, “Let me die.” Now death was lovely and beautiful in my sight, for I saw that #emph[we shall never live indeed till we have gone to the other world]. Oh, I thought, this life is only a slumber compared with that above. At this time also I saw more in those words, “heirs of God” (Rom 8:17), than I shall ever be able to express while I live in this world. “Heirs of God”! God Himself is the portion of the saints. This I saw and wondered at, but cannot tell you what I saw.

260\. Again, at another time, when I was very ill and weak, all that time also the tempter beset me strongly (for I find he is much for assaulting the soul when it begins to approach the grave; then is his opportunity), laboring to hide from me my former experience of God’s goodness, and also setting before me the terrors of death and the judgment of God. So much so that at this time, through my fear of perishing forever should I die now, I was as one dead before death came, and it was as if I had felt myself already descending into the pit. I thought, I say, that there was no way but that to hell I must go. But behold, just as I was in the midst of those fears, these words of the angels carrying Lazarus into Abraham’s bosom darted in upon me, as if to say, “So it shall be with you when you leave this world.” This sweetly revived my spirit, and helped me to hope in God. When I had mused on this with comfort for a while, that word fell with great weight upon my mind, “O death, where is your sting? O grave, where is your victory?” (1 Cor 15:55). At this I became well in both body and mind at once, for my sickness vanished at once, and I walked comfortably in my work for God again.

261\. At another time, though just before I had been fairly well and savory in my spirit, suddenly a great cloud of darkness fell upon me, which so hid from me the things of God and Christ that I was as if I had never seen or known them in my life. I was also so overrun in my soul with a senseless, heartless frame of spirit that I could not feel my soul move or stir after grace and life by Christ; I was as if my loins were broken, or as if my hands and feet had been tied or bound with chains. At this time also I felt some weakness seize upon my outward man, which made the other affliction all the more heavy and uncomfortable to me.

262\. After I had been in this condition some three or four days, as I was sitting by the fire, I suddenly felt this word sound in my heart, “I must go to Jesus.” At this my former darkness and atheism fled away, and the blessed things of heaven were set within my view. While I was thus overtaken with surprise so suddenly, “Wife,” I said, “is there ever such a scripture, ‘I must go to Jesus’?” She said she could not tell; so I sat musing still, to see if I could remember such a place. I had not sat more than two or three minutes when this came bolting in upon me, “to myriads of angels,” and with it, Hebrews the twelfth, about Mount Zion, was set before my eyes (vv. 22–24).

263\. Then with joy I told my wife, “Oh, now I know, I know!” That night was a good night to me; I have had only a few better. I longed for the company of some of God’s people, that I might have shared with them what God had shown me. Christ was a precious Christ to my soul that night; I could scarcely lie in my bed for joy, and peace, and triumph, through Christ. This great glory did not continue upon me until morning, yet the twelfth chapter of the author to the Hebrews (Heb 12:22–24) was a blessed scripture to me for many days together after this.

264\. The words are these: “You#super[+] have come to Mount Zion, to the city of the living God, the heavenly Jerusalem. You#super[+] have come to myriads of angels in joyful assembly, to the congregation of the firstborn, enrolled in heaven. You#super[+] have come to God the Judge of all, to the spirits of the righteous made perfect, to Jesus the mediator of a new covenant, and to the sprinkled blood that speaks a better word than the blood of Abel.” Through this blessed sentence the Lord led me over and over, first to this word, and then to that, and showed me wonderful glory in every one of them. These words also have often since this time been a great refreshment to my spirit. Blessed be God for having mercy on me.
